WN.lb(21,{"mt_f9pqp4Tjny":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is smaller: a mouse or a horse?","choices":["Mouse","Horse","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one could fit in your hand?","explain":"A mouse is small and takes up little room. A horse is big."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Two towers stand on the floor. One has {n} blocks and one has 1 block. Which is shorter?","choices":["The one with 1 block","The one with more blocks","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both start on the floor. Which one stays low?","explain":"Both start on the floor, so the 1-block tower stays lower. It is shorter than the {n}-block tower.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A short tower is on a chair. A tall tower is on the floor. Which tower is taller?","choices":["The one on the floor","The one on the chair","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"The chair lifts a tower up. It does not add blocks.","explain":"The chair only lifts the short tower up high. Put both on the floor and the floor tower is taller."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"To see which of two crayons is longer, what do you do first?","choices":["Line up the ends","Pick the one that sticks out","Pick your best colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is only fair when both start at the same place.","explain":"Line up the starts. Then the one that goes further at the other end is longer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A train has {n} carriages. Another train has 1 carriage. Which train is longer?","choices":["The one with more carriages","The one with 1 carriage","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each carriage makes a train go further along the track.","explain":"The train with {n} carriages goes further along, so it is longer than the train with 1.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word fits a giraffe?","choices":["Tall","Long","Small"],"answer":0,"hint":"A giraffe stands up, and its head is way up high.","explain":"A giraffe stands up high, so tall is the best word. Long is for things that lie along."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Three bowls: small, middle and big. Which one goes last in a small-to-big line?","choices":["The big one","The small one","The middle one"],"answer":0,"hint":"The line ends with the one that takes up most room.","explain":"Small to big ends with the biggest bowl: small, then middle, then big."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is bigger: a bus or a ball?","choices":["Bus","Ball","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one takes up lots of room on the road?","explain":"A bus is big. It takes up much more room than a ball, which is small."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Your tower has {n} blocks. Bear's tower has 2 blocks. Whose tower is taller?","choices":["Mine","Bear's","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"More blocks stacked up make a tower go higher.","explain":"Your {n} blocks go up higher than Bear's 2 blocks, so your tower is taller.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A worm goes on and on along the ground. Which word fits?","choices":["Long","Tall","Heavy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tall is for things that stand up. What word is for things that lie along?","explain":"A worm lies along the ground, so we say it is long. Tall is for things that go up high."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Tom's tower has {n} blocks. Sam's tower has {n} blocks too, but it stands on a box. Whose tower is taller?","choices":["They are the same","Sam's, it is on a box","Tom's"],"answer":0,"hint":"The box lifts Sam's tower up. Does it add any blocks?","explain":"Both towers have {n} blocks, so they are the same height. The box only lifts Sam's tower up high.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["An elephant is big. A mouse is small. Big things take up lots of room. Small things take up a bit of room. A giraffe is tall. It goes up high. A cat is short. Tall is for things that stand up.","A snake can be long. A long thing goes on and on. To see which crayon is long, put the ends side by side at a line. Then look at the far end.","A box can trick you. A short tower on a box looks tall. Put both towers on the floor, then look.","Line up three bears. Start with the small one. Then the middle one. Then the big one."],"adult":["At two and three, children learn to compare two things side by side and say how they differ: big and small for size, tall and short for height, long and short for length. Then they put three things in order by size, which is the start of measuring. The exact word matters. Tall is for height when something stands up. Long is for length along the ground. Big is the general word for size.","The lab gives each idea its own action. Children tap the big or small one of a pair, add and remove blocks to make a tower taller and then shorter than Bear's, slide a crayon back to the start line before choosing the longer one, move a tower off a box before comparing, and put three bears in order from smallest to biggest.","Common mistakes to watch for: judging length by which end sticks out further when the starts are not lined up; judging height by whose top is higher when one thing stands on a box or step; using big for every comparison instead of tall or long; and, when ordering three, finding the biggest and smallest but not checking the middle one. Correct these by lining things up together, not by telling.","Keep the language comparative and concrete: this one is longer, that one is shorter. Children of this age compare by looking side by side; numbers and rulers come later, so counting blocks here is only a check."]},"familyTip":"At bath or snack time, hold up two things, such as two spoons or two socks, and ask, \"Which is longer?\" Put their ends together on the table first. Then line up three cups or teddies from small to big and let your child check by lining up the bottoms.","keyIdea":"Big things take up lots of room and small things take up a little. Tall things go up high, and long things go a long way along. To compare fairly, start both things at the same line, then look at the other end. Put three things in order by starting with the smallest.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Big or small? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🐘 Big","🐭 Small"],"items":[["bus",0],["house",0],["whale",0],["tree",0],["ant",1],["pea",1],["bee",1],["button",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsize","caption":"Flagship lab: tap big and small, build a tower taller then shorter, line up crayon ends, take a tower off its box, and order three bears.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_fjf_vS04hs":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Water comes right up to the top of the cup. The cup is…","choices":["Full","Empty","Half full"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at where the water stops.","explain":"Water right to the top means the cup is full."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"One side of the scale goes up. The thing on that side is…","choices":["Lighter","Heavier","Bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which side does the heavy thing pull?","explain":"The heavy thing pulls its side down, so the side that goes up has the lighter thing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A teddy balances {n} blocks on the scale. How many blocks is the teddy as heavy as?","answer":"n","hint":"When the scale is level, both sides are just as heavy.","explain":"The scale is level with {n} blocks, so the teddy is as heavy as {n} blocks.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A cup has a little water at the bottom. Is it empty?","choices":["No, there is still some water","Yes, it is empty","Yes, it is full"],"answer":0,"hint":"Empty means no water at all. Is there any left?","explain":"A little water is still water, so the cup is not empty. Empty means nothing inside."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is lighter to hold: a leaf or a pumpkin?","choices":["The leaf","The pumpkin","You cannot tell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one would pull your hand down?","explain":"The pumpkin pulls your hand down. The leaf hardly does, so the leaf is lighter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The water stops at the middle of the jar. The jar is…","choices":["Half full","Full","Empty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it at the top, at the middle, or gone?","explain":"Water up to the middle means half full."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A big empty box and a small hammer. Which pulls the scale down?","choices":["The small hammer","The big empty box","Neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big is not the same as heavy.","explain":"The hammer is small but heavy. The empty box is big but light, so the hammer side goes down."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"The bucket is full after {n} scoops of sand. How many scoops do you pour in to make it full?","answer":"n","hint":"Full means the sand reaches the top. Count the scoops until it does.","explain":"It takes {n} scoops for the sand to reach the top, so {n} scoops make the bucket full.","params":{"n":[3,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"On the scale the book side goes down and the feather side goes up. Which is heavy and which is light?","choices":["The book is heavy, the feather is light","The feather is heavy, the book is light","They are just as heavy"],"answer":0,"hint":"The heavy side pulls the scale down.","explain":"The book side went down, so the book is heavy. The feather side went up, so the feather is light."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The apple balances {n} blocks. The scale is level. How many blocks is the apple as heavy as?","answer":"n","hint":"Level means both sides are just as heavy.","explain":"The scale is level, so the apple is as heavy as the {n} blocks.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is heavier: a big balloon or a small stone?","choices":["The small stone","The big balloon","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big is not the same as heavy. Think about holding them.","explain":"The balloon is big but full of air. The small stone pulls your hand down more, so it is heavier."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A full jug holds {n} cups of water. You pour out all {n} cups. How many cups of water are left in the jug?","answer":"n-n","hint":"You poured out every cup. What is left?","explain":"All {n} cups came out, so 0 are left. The jug is empty.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Hold a book in one hand and a feather in the other. The book pulls your hand down. The book is heavy. The feather is light.","A scale is like two hands. Put a thing on each side. The heavy side goes down. The light side goes up.","Big is not the same as heavy. A big balloon is full of air, so it is light. A small stone is heavy. Feel it or use the scale to find out.","Pour water in a cup. When it reaches the top, the cup is full. Pour it all out and the cup is empty. Water up to the middle means half full."],"adult":["This is the start of measures in the early years. Development Matters for 3 and 4 year olds asks children to make comparisons between objects relating to size, length, weight and capacity. In Year 1 the National Curriculum names the words: mass or weight (heavy, light, heavier than, lighter than) and capacity (full, empty, more than, less than, half full). US Common Core K.MD.A.2 asks children to compare two objects and say which has more of a measurable attribute.","At this age children compare directly, with no units: hefting one object in each hand, then using a simple balance, where the heavier pan goes down. Capacity is learned through water and sand play, filling and emptying containers and using full, empty and half full. Measuring with cubes or cups comes later.","Common mistakes: believing the bigger object must be heavier (size and mass are confused), reading the balance the wrong way so that the side that rises is called heavier, calling a cup full when it is nearly full, and calling a cup empty when a little is left. Ask 'how do you know?' so the child points to the scale or the top of the cup.","In the lab your child drags things onto a balance and picks the heavier or lighter one, tests big light things against small heavy ones, pours water to full, empty and half full against a halfway line, and names how full a jar, bowl or cup is."]},"familyTip":"At bath time give your child two cups and ask for one full and one empty, then one half full. At the shops let them hold two bags and say which is heavier, then check together with the scales.","keyIdea":"Hold one thing in each hand: the heavy one pulls your hand down. On a balance scale the heavier side goes down and the lighter side goes up. Big things are not always heavy: a big balloon is lighter than a small stone. A cup is full when the water reaches the top, empty when there is none left, and half full when the water stops at the middle.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Heavy or light? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🧱 Heavy","🪶 Light"],"items":[["brick",0],["pumpkin",0],["big bag of potatoes",0],["tin of beans",0],["feather",1],["leaf",1],["balloon",1],["tissue",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagheavy","caption":"Flagship lab: find the heavier and lighter thing on a balance, test big light things, and pour a cup full, empty and half full.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_WtcFrxGOgw":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A square has sides of {s} cm. What is its perimeter in cm?","answer":"4*s","hint":"A square has 4 equal sides. Add {s} four times.","explain":"{s} + {s} + {s} + {s} = {=4*s}, so the perimeter is {=4*s} cm.","params":{"s":[3,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A triangle has a perimeter of {=a+b+c} cm. Two sides are {a} cm and {b} cm. How long is the third side in cm?","answer":"c","hint":"Add the two sides you know, then take that from {=a+b+c}.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, and {=a+b+c} − {=a+b} = {c}. The third side is {c} cm.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[3,9],"c":[3,9]},"where":"c<a+b && a<b+c && b<a+c"},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A rectangle is 9 cm by 2 cm. Mo says the perimeter is 18 cm. What went wrong?","choices":["He found the area, 9 × 2, not the edge","Nothing, 18 cm is right","He added only two sides"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 9 × 2 the distance round the edge or the squares inside?","explain":"9 × 2 = 18 is the area in squares. The perimeter is 9 + 2 + 9 + 2 = 22 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A rectangle has a perimeter of 30 cm. One side is 10 cm. How long is the other side?","choices":["5 cm","20 cm","10 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"There are two sides of 10 cm. Take both off, then halve what is left.","explain":"30 − 10 − 10 = 10 cm for the two other sides, so each is 5 cm. 20 cm takes off only one side, and 10 cm forgets to halve."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"12 squares are laid out as a 12 by 1 strip and as a 4 by 3 rectangle. Which has the longer perimeter?","choices":["The 12 by 1 strip","The 4 by 3 rectangle","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the four sides of each one.","explain":"12 + 1 + 12 + 1 = 26 cm and 4 + 3 + 4 + 3 = 14 cm. Same area, but the strip has the longer perimeter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"An octagon has 8 equal sides of 3 cm. What is its perimeter?","choices":["24 cm","11 cm","18 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the sides: 8 of them, each 3 cm.","explain":"8 × 3 = 24, so the perimeter is 24 cm. 11 cm adds 8 and 3, and 18 cm counts only 6 sides."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A quadrilateral has a perimeter of 26 cm. Three sides are 9 cm, 4 cm and 7 cm. How long is the fourth side?","choices":["6 cm","20 cm","46 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the three sides you know, then subtract from 26.","explain":"9 + 4 + 7 = 20, and 26 − 20 = 6 cm. 20 cm is the known sides, and 46 cm adds the perimeter on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which unit fits the perimeter of a playground?","choices":["metres (m)","square metres (m²)","kilograms (kg)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Perimeter is a length: how far round the edge.","explain":"Perimeter is a distance, so it is measured in metres. Square metres measure area, and kilograms measure mass."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A rectangle is {a} cm long and {b} cm wide. What is its perimeter in cm?","answer":"2*a+2*b","hint":"Add all four sides: {a} + {b} + {a} + {b}.","explain":"{a} + {b} + {a} + {b} = {=2*a+2*b}, so the perimeter is {=2*a+2*b} cm.","params":{"a":[4,12],"b":[2,9]},"where":"a>b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A rectangle has a perimeter of {=2*a+2*b} cm. One side is {a} cm. How long is the other side in cm?","answer":"b","hint":"Take off both {a} cm sides, then halve what is left.","explain":"{=2*a+2*b} − {a} − {a} = {=2*b}, and half of {=2*b} is {b}. The other side is {b} cm.","params":{"a":[5,12],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A pentagon has sides of 6 cm, 4 cm, 5 cm, 7 cm and 3 cm. What is its perimeter?","choices":["25 cm","22 cm","5 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pentagon has 5 sides. Tick each one as you add it.","explain":"6 + 4 + 5 + 7 + 3 = 25, so the perimeter is 25 cm. 22 cm misses the 3 cm side, and 5 cm is just the number of sides."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A 3 by 7 rectangle and a 5 by 5 square both have a perimeter of 20 cm. Which has the bigger area?","choices":["The 5 by 5 square","The 3 by 7 rectangle","They have the same area"],"answer":0,"hint":"Area is the squares inside: multiply length by width for each one.","explain":"3 + 7 + 3 + 7 = 20 and 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20, so the perimeters match. But 5 × 5 = 25 squares and 3 × 7 = 21 squares, so the square has the bigger area."}],"explain":{"kid":["Put your finger on one corner of a shape. Run it along every side until you get back to the start. The distance your finger went is the perimeter. For a rectangle 8 cm long and 5 cm wide, that is 8 + 5 + 8 + 5 = 26 cm.","A polygon is a flat shape with straight sides. To find its perimeter, add up all its sides. Count the sides first, so you do not miss one. A hexagon has 6 sides. If each one is 4 cm, the perimeter is 6 × 4 = 24 cm.","Sometimes one side is missing. A pentagon has a perimeter of 30 cm, and four of its sides add up to 26 cm. The last side must be 30 − 26 = 4 cm. For a rectangle with perimeter 24 cm and one side of 8 cm, take off both 8 cm sides. That leaves 8 cm for the two other sides, so each is 4 cm.","Perimeter is not the same as area. Area counts the squares inside. A 6 by 6 square and an 11 by 1 strip both have a perimeter of 24 cm. But the square holds 36 squares and the strip holds only 11."],"adult":["This is US Common Core 3.MD.D.8. Children solve problems with the perimeters of polygons. They find the perimeter from side lengths, find an unknown side, and show rectangles with the same perimeter but different areas, or the same area but different perimeters. In England, Year 3 pupils measure the perimeter of simple 2-D shapes. Year 4 pupils measure and work out the perimeter of a rectilinear figure, including squares, in cm and m.","Perimeter is a length, so it is measured in cm or m. Area is measured in square units. For a rectangle, perimeter = 2 × (length + width). Let children add all four sides first, so the formula means something. Finding a missing side is the inverse: take the known sides from the perimeter. For a rectangle, take off both known sides and then halve what is left.","Common mistakes: counting the squares inside, which gives the area; adding only the two labelled sides of a rectangle; taking a known side off only once when finding a missing side; forgetting to halve at the end; adding the perimeter to the known sides; and miscounting the sides of a polygon. A careful check is to walk round the shape and tick each side as it is added.","In the lab your child walks round an 8 cm by 5 cm rectangle side by side, finds missing sides of a pentagon, a quadrilateral and a regular hexagon, solves rectangles from their perimeter, drags the corner of a rectangle that keeps a perimeter of 24 cm while its area changes, and builds every rectangle from 12 squares to compare their perimeters."]},"familyTip":"Use a piece of string to go round a book, a table mat or a door, then measure the string with a ruler or tape measure. Ask which object has the longest edge. Then lay 12 coins or cards in different rectangles and count the edge of each.","keyIdea":"The perimeter is the total distance all the way round the edge of a flat shape. Add the lengths of every side and keep the unit, such as cm or m. A rectangle has two pairs of equal sides, so its perimeter is length + width + length + width. If you know the perimeter and every side but one, subtract the sides you know. Two rectangles can have the same perimeter but different areas, or the same area but different perimeters.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it about the perimeter or the area? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Perimeter (the edge)","Area (the inside)"],"items":[["A fence round a garden",0],["Grass seed to cover a lawn",1],["Ribbon round a card",0],["Carpet for a floor",1],["A frame round a picture",0],["Paint for a wall",1],["Tape round a box lid",0],["Tiles on a bathroom floor",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagperim","caption":"Flagship lab: walk round a rectangle, find missing sides, stretch a 24 cm rectangle and compare shapes made of 12 squares.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0XxyaQLRhn":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which hand tells the hour?","choices":["The short hand","The long hand","Both hands"],"answer":0,"hint":"One hand is short and one is long. Which one points to the hour?","explain":"The short hand is the hour hand. The long hand shows the minutes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The long hand is on 6. The short hand is between 8 and 9. What time is it?","choices":["Half past 8","Half past 9","6 o'clock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which number has the short hand just gone past?","explain":"The short hand has just passed 8 and the long hand is on 6, so it is half past 8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where is the long hand at 7 o'clock?","choices":["On the 12","On the 7","On the 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"O'clock times always have the long hand in the same place.","explain":"At any o'clock time the long hand points to 12. The short hand points to 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Where is the short hand at half past 2?","choices":["Halfway between 2 and 3","Right on the 2","On the 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"The short hand keeps moving while the long hand turns.","explain":"At half past 2 the short hand has moved halfway from 2 towards 3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"It is {a} o'clock. The long hand goes all the way round {n} times. What o'clock is it now?","answer":"a+n","hint":"Each full turn of the long hand is 1 hour. Count on {n} hours from {a}.","explain":"{n} full turns is {n} hours, so {a} o'clock becomes {=a+n} o'clock.","params":{"a":[1,6],"n":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"How long does the long hand take to go all the way round?","choices":["1 hour","1 minute","12 hours"],"answer":0,"hint":"One full turn of the long hand moves the short hand on one number.","explain":"One full turn of the long hand takes 1 hour, which is 60 minutes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"The long hand is on 12 and the short hand is on {h}. It is ___ o'clock.","answer":"h","hint":"With the long hand on 12, read the number the short hand points to.","explain":"The short hand points to {h}, so it is {h} o'clock.","params":{"h":[1,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam says the clock shows 12 o'clock. The long hand is on 12 and the short hand is on 3. What did Sam do?","choices":["Read the hands the wrong way round","Read it right","Forgot the half past"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which hand should Sam look at for the hour?","explain":"Sam read the long hand as the hour. The short hand is on 3, so it is 3 o'clock."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The long hand is on 12. The short hand is on 4. What time is it?","choices":["4 o'clock","12 o'clock","Half past 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"The short hand tells the hour. The long hand on 12 means o'clock.","explain":"The short hand points to 4 and the long hand is on 12, so it is 4 o'clock."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"It is {h} o'clock. One hour passes. What o'clock is it now?","answer":"h+1","hint":"In one hour the short hand moves on to the next number.","explain":"One hour after {h} o'clock it is {=h+1} o'clock. The long hand went all the way round once.","params":{"h":[1,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"It is half past {h}. The short hand is between {h} and which number?","answer":"h+1","hint":"At half past, the short hand is halfway to the next number.","explain":"At half past {h} the short hand is halfway between {h} and {=h+1}.","params":{"h":[1,11]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The long hand is on 6. What do we say?","choices":["Half past","O'clock","6 o'clock"],"answer":0,"hint":"The long hand has gone half way round the clock.","explain":"The long hand on 6 means half past. Then read the hour from the short hand."}],"explain":{"kid":["A clock has two hands. The short hand tells the hour. The long hand tells the minutes.","When the long hand points to 12, we say o'clock. Look where the short hand points. On the 3? It is 3 o'clock.","When the long hand goes all the way round, one hour has passed. The short hand moves on to the next number.","When the long hand points to 6, we say half past. The short hand is halfway to the next number. Just past 4? It is half past 4."],"adult":["In England this is Year 1 Measurement in the National Curriculum: tell the time to the hour and half past the hour, and draw the hands on a clock face to show these times. Children also learn the language before and after, and sequence events in time. In the US, Common Core 1.MD.B.3 asks first graders to tell and write time in hours and half-hours using analogue and digital clocks.","Why the hour hand moves: both hands turn all the time. The minute hand goes once round the face in 60 minutes, and in that time the hour hand travels from one number to the next. So at half past, the hour hand is halfway between two numbers, and the hour you say is the number it has just passed.","Common mistakes to watch for: children often read the hands the wrong way round, so 3 o'clock becomes 12 o'clock; they draw the hour hand right on the number at half past; at half past 4 they say half past 5 because the short hand is close to the 5; and they read the long hand on 6 as 6 o'clock. Always ask: which hand is the short one, and which number has it just passed?","In the lab your child turns the long hand once round and watches the short hand move on one number, reads three o'clock times, drags the short hand to make 7 o'clock and 2 o'clock, turns the long hand to the 6 to make half past 4, and reads three half-past clocks. Every step works by dragging on the clock or with big buttons."]},"familyTip":"Pick two times in your day, like 8 o'clock for school and half past 6 for dinner. At those times, look at a real clock together and ask: where is the long hand? Where is the short hand?","keyIdea":"An analogue clock has two hands. The short hand is the hour hand: it points to the hour. The long hand is the minute hand: when it points to 12 the time is o'clock, and when it points to 6 it is half past. While the long hand goes all the way round once, one hour passes and the short hand moves on one number, so at half past the short hand sits halfway between two numbers. Half past 4 means the short hand has just passed the 4, not reached the 5.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"O'clock or half past? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["O'clock","Half past"],"items":[["Long hand on 12",0],["Long hand on 6",1],["Short hand right on a number",0],["Short hand between two numbers",1],["3 o'clock",0],["Half past 3",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagclock","caption":"Flagship lab: turn the long hand to move the hour, read o'clock and half-past clocks, and drag the hands to set a time.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_-vvVxpOHG2":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Today is Monday. What day was yesterday?","choices":["Sunday","Tuesday","Monday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Yesterday is the day before today. What day comes before Monday?","explain":"Yesterday was the day before Monday: Sunday. Tuesday will be tomorrow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which comes first?","choices":["The seed","The flower","They come together"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you put in the soil to make a plant grow?","explain":"The seed comes first. Next a shoot grows, and last the flower opens."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"When do we usually have a bath and go to bed?","choices":["In the evening","In the morning","After breakfast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it at the start of the day or the end?","explain":"Bath and bed come in the evening, at the end of the day."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"\"Eat your lunch after you wash your hands.\" What do you do first?","choices":["Wash my hands","Eat my lunch","Both at once"],"answer":0,"hint":"After tells you which job is later. Which job is after washing?","explain":"You hear 'eat' first, but 'after you wash' means you wash first, then eat."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You made a snowman on day {d}. It melted {w} days later. On which day did it melt?","answer":"d+w","hint":"Start at day {d} and count on {w} more days.","explain":"Day {d} and {w} more days is day {=d+w}. It melted after you built it.","params":{"d":[1,3],"w":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Your party is the day after today. When is it?","choices":["Tomorrow","Yesterday","Today"],"answer":0,"hint":"Has the party happened yet, or is it still to come?","explain":"The day after today is tomorrow. It has not happened yet."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Today is day {d} of the holiday. What day of the holiday was yesterday?","answer":"d-1","hint":"Yesterday is one day before today. Count back one.","explain":"Yesterday was the day before today, so it was day {=d-1}.","params":{"d":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is in the right order?","choices":["Wake up, eat breakfast, go to bed","Go to bed, wake up, eat breakfast","Eat breakfast, go to bed, wake up"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you do at the very start of the day?","explain":"First you wake up, next you eat breakfast, and last, in the evening, you go to bed."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What comes straight after morning?","choices":["Afternoon","Evening","Night"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lunch is in the middle of the day. What part comes after lunch?","explain":"Morning, then afternoon, then evening. Afternoon comes straight after morning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} pictures in a story. You have put {k} of them in order. How many are left to put in order?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start at {n} and take away the {k} you have done.","explain":"{n} pictures take away {k} done leaves {=n-k} to put in order.","params":{"n":[4,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Today is day {d} of the holiday. What day of the holiday will tomorrow be?","answer":"d+1","hint":"Tomorrow is one day after today. Count on one.","explain":"Tomorrow is the day after today, so it is day {=d+1}.","params":{"d":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"\"Before you go out, put your coat on.\" What do you do first?","choices":["Put my coat on","Go out","Take my coat off"],"answer":0,"hint":"Before tells you which job is earlier. Which job is before going out?","explain":"You hear 'go out' first, but 'before' means the coat goes on first, then you go out."}],"explain":{"kid":["Things happen in order. First you plant a seed. Next a shoot grows. Last you get a flower.","Before means it comes first. After means it comes next. Socks go on before shoes. Shoes go on after socks.","A day has parts. In the morning we eat breakfast. In the afternoon we play. In the evening we have a bath and go to bed.","Today is now. Yesterday is the day that has gone. Tomorrow is the day that has not come yet."],"adult":["In England this is Year 1 measurement in the National Curriculum: pupils should be taught to sequence events in chronological order using language such as before and after, next, first, today, yesterday, tomorrow, morning, afternoon and evening. It grows out of the EYFS reception goal of describing a sequence of events using words like first and then, and it leads on to days of the week, months and telling the time.","The key point is that time words describe the order things really happen in, not the order you say them. 'Eat your lunch after you wash your hands' mentions eating first, but you wash first. Yesterday and tomorrow are both 'not today', but one is behind you and one is ahead.","Common mistakes to watch for: young children often follow the order they hear, so 'before' and 'after' sentences get done back to front; they jump from morning straight to evening and forget the afternoon; and they swap yesterday and tomorrow, or use 'yesterday' for any time in the past. Asking 'has it happened yet?' sorts yesterday from tomorrow better than explaining.","In the lab your child drags picture cards into slots: a seed growing (first, next, last), two jobs given with before and after, the parts of a day as the sun moves across the sky, a three-day strip with today in the middle, and a four-picture snowman story. Every card can also be placed with a big button. Read each sentence aloud together."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, ask your child to tell you three things from today in order, using first, next and last. Then ask: what will happen tomorrow? Try a 'before and after' game while dressing: 'Before your coat, what goes on?'","keyIdea":"Things happen in an order. Time words tell us that order. First, next and last say which thing comes when. Before means earlier and after means later. A day goes morning, then afternoon, then evening. Yesterday was the day before today, and tomorrow is the day after today.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Morning or evening? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Morning","Evening"],"items":[["Eat breakfast",0],["Wake up",0],["Get dressed",0],["Have a bath",1],["Read a bedtime story",1],["Put on pyjamas",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagorder","caption":"Flagship lab: drag picture cards into order with first, next, last, before and after, morning to evening, and yesterday, today and tomorrow.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_6xNmQLzuqm":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A shape is covered by {a} rows of unit squares with {b} squares in each row. What is its area in square units?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Each row has {b} squares. Add one row at a time.","explain":"{a} rows of {b} make {=a*b} unit squares, so the area is {=a*b} square units.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[3,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A shape has an area of {a} square units. You move one of its squares to a new spot that still touches the shape. What is the area now?","answer":"a","hint":"Did you add a square or take one away?","explain":"The same {a} unit squares are still there, just in a different place, so the area is still {a} square units. Only the perimeter can change.","params":{"a":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What does 'one square unit' mean?","choices":["The area of a square with sides 1 unit long","A line 1 unit long","Any tile, whatever its size"],"answer":0,"hint":"A unit square has sides of 1 unit.","explain":"One square unit is the area of a unit square, a square with each side 1 unit long. A line 1 unit long is a length, not an area."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ava covers a rectangle with 8 tiles, but some tiles are big and some are small. Is its area 8 square units?","choices":["No, the tiles must all be the same unit square","Yes, 8 tiles is 8 square units","Yes, if the big tiles are at the edge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would a big tile and a small tile count as the same amount of space?","explain":"No. Mixed sizes do not give a fair count, because a big tile covers more space than a small one. Use equal unit squares."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Shape A is 6 squares long and 1 square wide. Shape B is 3 squares long and 2 squares wide. Which has the bigger area?","choices":["They have the same area, 6 square units","Shape A, because it is longer","Shape B, because it is wider"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the unit squares in each shape.","explain":"A has 6 squares and B has 2 rows of 3, which is also 6. Both are 6 square units. Looking longer does not mean more area."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A shape has 5 whole squares and 4 half squares. Leo says the area is 9 square units. What should it be?","choices":["7 square units","9 square units","5 square units"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two half squares make one whole square.","explain":"The 4 halves make 2 whole squares, so the area is 5 + 2 = 7 square units. Leo counted each half as a whole."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A rectangle is 4 squares long and 2 squares wide. Which is its area?","choices":["8 square units","12 units","6 square units"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the squares inside, not the length of the edge.","explain":"2 rows of 4 make 8 unit squares, so the area is 8 square units. 12 units is the perimeter, the distance round the edge, and 6 adds the two sides."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A rectangle is {a} squares long and {b} squares wide. How many square units is its area?","answer":"a*b","hint":"It has {b} rows, and each row has {a} squares. Count them all, or add the rows.","explain":"{b} rows of {a} unit squares make {=a*b} squares, so the area is {=a*b} square units.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A shape is made of {a} whole squares and {=2*b} half squares. What is its area in square units?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Two half squares make one whole square. How many wholes do the {=2*b} halves make?","explain":"{=2*b} halves make {b} whole squares. {a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so the area is {=a+b} square units.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Kai covers a shape with 9 circles and says its area is 9 square units. What is wrong?","choices":["Circles leave gaps, so they do not measure area fairly","Nothing, 9 circles is 9 square units","He should have counted the edge instead"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can circles fit together with no gaps between them?","explain":"Circles leave gaps, so some of the shape is not counted. Area must be measured with unit squares that fit with no gaps and no overlaps."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You want to buy a fence to go round a garden. What do you need to measure?","choices":["The perimeter","The area","The number of plants"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the fence cover the garden, or go round its edge?","explain":"A fence goes round the edge, so you need the perimeter. Area would tell you how much grass or soil is inside."}],"explain":{"kid":["Area is how much flat space a shape covers. Think of the floor of your room. A rug that covers more of the floor has a bigger area.","To measure area we use a unit square. It is a square with sides 1 unit long, like 1 cm. Its area is 1 square unit. Cover the shape with unit squares and count them. A rectangle 4 squares long and 3 squares wide holds 3 rows of 4, so its area is 12 square units.","The count is only fair if the squares are all the same size, with no gaps and no overlaps. Circles leave gaps. Tiles on top of each other get counted twice. If you move a square to a new spot, the area stays the same, but the edge can get longer.","Area is not perimeter. Perimeter is the length all the way round the edge, like a fence. Area is the space inside, like a carpet. Some shapes have half squares. Two halves make one whole square unit, so count the wholes and then pair up the halves."],"adult":["The US Common Core starts area in Grade 3. It says 'a square with side length 1 unit, called \"a unit square,\" is said to have \"one square unit\" of area'. A shape that can be covered 'without gaps or overlaps by n unit squares' has an area of n square units (3.MD.5). Children measure it 'by counting unit squares' (3.MD.6). In England, Year 4 pupils 'find the area of rectilinear shapes by counting squares'. Year 5 then works out the area of rectangles in cm² and m².","Area measures flat space, so the unit must be an area too. Counting only works when the units fit the shape exactly. They must be equal squares, with no gaps and no overlaps. Seeing a rectangle as rows of equal squares (3 rows of 4) leads to length × width later. If you move the same squares into a new shape, the area stays the same, even when the perimeter changes.","Common mistakes: mixing up area and perimeter (counting the squares round the edge, or adding the sides); counting mixed sizes, circles or overlapping tiles as if they were unit squares; thinking a longer shape must have more area; counting each half square as a whole, or leaving halves out; and giving no unit, or cm instead of square cm.","In the lab learners drag across a grid to cover shapes. They judge whether a tiling gives a fair count. They move one square and see the area stay put while the perimeter changes. They decide whether a job needs area or perimeter, and they pair up half squares."]},"familyTip":"Use sticky notes or square crackers as unit squares. Cover a book cover or a tray and count them, then ask: would it be fair to use some big and some small ones? Move one cracker to stick out at the side and ask whether there is more cracker now.","keyIdea":"Area is the amount of flat surface a shape covers. We measure it in unit squares: a square with sides of 1 unit has an area of 1 square unit. Cover the shape with unit squares, with no gaps and no overlaps, and count them. Area is different from perimeter, which is the distance round the edge.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the job need area or perimeter? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Area","Perimeter"],"items":[["Carpet for a bedroom floor",0],["A fence round a field",1],["Paint for a wall",0],["A border round a noticeboard",1],["Grass seed for a lawn",0],["A frame round a photo",1],["Wrapping paper for the top of a box",0],["Lace round the edge of a tablecloth",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcover","caption":"Flagship lab: cover shapes with unit squares, judge fair tilings, move a square, tell area from perimeter, pair half squares.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_bj1YCgNWUx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many days are in one week?","choices":["7","5","12"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the days from Monday to Sunday and count them on your fingers.","explain":"Monday to Sunday is 7 days. School days may be 5, but Saturday and Sunday count too. 12 is the months in a year."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many months are in a year?","choices":["12","7","52"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the months from January to December and count them.","explain":"January to December is 12 months. 7 is the days in a week, and 52 is about the weeks in a year."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Today is Saturday. What day was yesterday?","choices":["Friday","Sunday","Thursday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Yesterday is the day just before today.","explain":"The day before Saturday is Friday. Sunday is tomorrow, and Thursday was two days ago."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which month comes after June?","choices":["July","May","August"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the months in order: April, May, June, then what?","explain":"July comes straight after June. May comes before June, and August is after July."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Today is Thursday. What day will it be in 3 days?","choices":["Sunday","Saturday","Monday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do not count today. Hop: Friday is 1, then keep going.","explain":"Friday is 1, Saturday is 2, Sunday is 3. Saturday comes from counting Thursday as 1, and Monday is one hop too many."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is longer: a week or a month?","choices":["a month","a week","they are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how many weeks fit in one month.","explain":"A month has 28 to 31 days, which is a bit more than 4 weeks. A week is only 7 days."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A school trip is in {a} weeks. Each week has 7 days. How many days away is the trip?","answer":"a*7","hint":"Each week is 7 days. Count on 7 for each week.","explain":"{a} weeks of 7 days is {=a*7} days, so the trip is {=a*7} days away.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"The shop is open {a} days a week. How many days a week is it closed?","answer":"7-a","hint":"A week has 7 days. Take away the days it is open.","explain":"7 − {a} = {=7-a}, so the shop is closed {=7-a} days a week.","params":{"a":[3,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Today is Wednesday. What day will it be tomorrow?","choices":["Thursday","Tuesday","Friday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tomorrow is the day after today. Say the days in order from Wednesday.","explain":"The day after Wednesday is Thursday. Tuesday was yesterday, and Friday is two days away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A week has 7 days. You have already had {a} days of this week. How many days of the week are left?","answer":"7-a","hint":"Start from 7 days and take away the days that have gone.","explain":"7 − {a} = {=7-a}, so {=7-a} days of the week are left. Then a new week starts.","params":{"a":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A year has 12 months. {a} months of this year have gone. How many months are left?","answer":"12-a","hint":"Start from 12 months and take away the months that have gone.","explain":"12 − {a} = {=12-a}, so {=12-a} months are left before a new year starts in January.","params":{"a":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which comes after Sunday?","choices":["Monday","Saturday","No day: the week is over"],"answer":0,"hint":"The days go round like a wheel. Where does the week start again?","explain":"After Sunday the week starts again, so Monday comes next. Saturday comes before Sunday."}],"explain":{"kid":["A week has 7 days. They go Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday. Then the week starts again with Monday.","Today is the day we are in now. Yesterday was the day before. Tomorrow is the day after. If today is Tuesday, yesterday was Monday and tomorrow is Wednesday.","To find the day in 2 days, start on today and hop. The first hop is tomorrow. Do not count today as a hop.","A month is much longer than a week. It is about 4 weeks. There are 12 months in a year. They go from January to December, then a new year starts. Your birthday comes once a year."],"adult":["England's Year 1 maths programme asks children to 'recognise and use language relating to dates, including days of the week, weeks, months and years', alongside sequencing events in time. Year 2 adds that there are 60 minutes in an hour and 24 hours in a day. The aim at this age is the names in order and how the units nest, not reading a full calendar.","The facts to hold: 7 days in a week, 12 months in a year, 28 to 31 days in a month, 365 days in a year (366 in a leap year), which is 52 weeks and 1 or 2 days. A month is therefore a little over 4 weeks. UK and ISO calendars start the week on Monday, and many US calendars start on Sunday, but the cycle is the same.","Common mistakes: thinking the week ends after Sunday instead of going round, saying 5 days in a week because of school days, mixing up 7 days and 12 months, swapping yesterday and tomorrow, and counting today as the first day when working out 'in 3 days'. Children also often think a month and a week are about the same length.","In the lab your child builds the week wheel and the year wheel by tapping the next name, turns a Today arrow past Sunday, hops on to find a day in a few days, and fills a 30-day April with whole weeks before filling a year with months."]},"familyTip":"Each morning, ask: what day is it today, what was yesterday, and what will tomorrow be? Hang a simple calendar low down and let your child cross off each day. Sing the days of the week or the months while you walk, and count down the days to something exciting by hopping on from tomorrow.","keyIdea":"Time comes in sizes. A day is one morning, afternoon, evening and night. Seven days make a week: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday and Sunday. About four weeks make a month, and twelve months, from January to December, make a year. The days and the months go round in the same order, so after Sunday comes Monday and after December comes January.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a day of the week or a month? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Day of the week","Month"],"items":[["Monday",0],["Friday",0],["Sunday",0],["Wednesday",0],["March",1],["July",1],["October",1],["December",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcal","caption":"Flagship lab: build the week wheel, turn past Sunday, hop on to a future day, build the year wheel and fill a month with weeks.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_TcG90kS8nu":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which takes the shortest time?","choices":["a sneeze","a night of sleep","a day at school"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is over almost as soon as it starts?","explain":"A sneeze is over in a moment. A night of sleep and a school day both go on for hours."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A song takes 6 claps. A dance takes 6 claps. Which is true?","choices":["they take the same time","the song takes longer","the dance takes longer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the number of claps for each one.","explain":"6 claps and 6 claps is the same number, so the song and the dance take the same time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The bath starts first. The snack starts later and ends last. How can you tell which takes longer?","choices":["count the claps for each one","the one that ends last takes longer","the one that starts first takes longer"],"answer":0,"hint":"The end alone does not tell you. What do you need to know about the start too?","explain":"The snack may end last only because it started late. Count the claps from each start, or line up the starts, then compare."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Two children race. Who is slower?","choices":["the one who takes more claps","the one who takes fewer claps","the taller child"],"answer":0,"hint":"More claps means more time.","explain":"Taking more claps means taking more time, so that child is slower. Being tall does not tell you the time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The sand timer runs out before the song ends. Which takes longer?","choices":["the song","the sand timer","they take the same time"],"answer":0,"hint":"They started together. Which one is still going?","explain":"The song is still playing when the sand has run out, so the song takes longer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Putting on shoes takes {a} claps. Putting on a coat takes {b} claps. How many claps for both, one after the other?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the claps for the shoes and the claps for the coat.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so both jobs together take {=a+b} claps.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A hop race takes {a} claps. A walk takes {b} claps more. How many claps does the walk take?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start at the hop race claps and count on.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. The walk takes more claps, so it takes a longer time.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Put these in order from the shortest time to the longest: lunch, sneeze, putting on shoes.","choices":["sneeze, shoes, lunch","lunch, shoes, sneeze","shoes, sneeze, lunch"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the quickest one first, then the next, then the longest.","explain":"A sneeze is the shortest, putting on shoes takes a little while, and lunch takes the longest."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which takes longer: eating your lunch or blinking your eyes?","choices":["eating your lunch","blinking your eyes","they take the same time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about starting both at the same time. Which one is still going?","explain":"A blink is over in a moment. Eating lunch keeps going for a long time, so it takes longer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A song takes {a} claps. Tidy up takes {b} claps. How many more claps does the song take?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Start from the bigger number of claps and count back the smaller number.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}, so the song takes {=a-b} more claps. More claps means a longer time.","params":{"a":[8,10],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Mia runs in {a} claps. Sam takes 2 more claps than Mia. How many claps does Sam take?","answer":"a+2","hint":"Start at Mia's claps and count on 2 more.","explain":"{a} + 2 = {=a+2}. Sam takes more claps, so Sam is slower and Mia is quicker.","params":{"a":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ben tidies up in 4 claps. Ava tidies up in 7 claps. Who is quicker?","choices":["Ben","Ava","they are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fewer claps means less time.","explain":"Ben takes 4 claps, fewer than 7, so Ben is quicker. Ava takes more time, so she is slower."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some things take a long time, like a night of sleep. Some take a short time, like a sneeze.","Start two things at the same time. Which one is still going when the other stops? That one takes longer.","We can count claps to measure time. Clap at the same speed. 6 claps and 6 claps is the same time. Fewer claps is quicker. More claps is slower.","Be careful. The one that ends last may have started late. Line up the starts, then look."],"adult":["England's Year 1 maths programme asks children to 'compare, describe and solve practical problems for time [for example, quicker, slower, earlier, later]' and to 'measure and begin to record time (hours, minutes, seconds)'. In the Early Years this starts as talk: which takes longer, which is shorter, the same time. Duration means how long an event lasts, from its start to its end.","Young children judge time the way they judge length: they compare by eye and by the end point. Just as two sticks must start at the same line to compare lengths, two events must start together, or be measured in the same units, before one can be called longer. Non-standard units such as claps, a sand timer or counting to ten come before seconds and minutes, and they only work if the beat stays steady.","Common mistakes: children often decide that the event which finishes last took the longest, even when it started later. In a race, many think more claps means quicker, but more claps means more time, so slower. Some mix up a long time with something big or far away. Others say two events are different when they take the same number of claps.","In the lab your child presses Play, so both bars grow together, and taps the one still going. Next they stretch a bar until two jobs take the same time. Then they slide a late snack back to the start line. Last, they pick the quicker runner by fewer claps and order three jobs from shortest to longest time."]},"familyTip":"Race small jobs against each other: who can put their shoes on before the song ends? Clap or count together while two things happen, then ask which took longer. Use the words quicker, slower, longer and shorter time, and a sand timer or a kitchen timer to check.","keyIdea":"A duration is how long something takes. To compare two durations, start them at the same time: the one still going when the other stops takes longer, and the other takes a shorter time. We can measure time by counting steady claps. The same number of claps means the same time. In a race, the one who takes fewer claps is quicker and the one who takes more is slower. Ending last does not mean taking longest if it started late.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it take a short time or a long time? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Short time","Long time"],"items":[["Blink",0],["Sneeze",0],["Clap once",0],["Blow out a candle",0],["Sleep at night",1],["A day at school",1],["Grow a tree",1],["A long car trip",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdur","caption":"Flagship lab: start two jobs together, make two jobs take the same time, line up a late start, pick the quicker runner and order three jobs by time.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HNOPGJYiRK":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many minutes from one number to the next on a clock?","choices":["5","1","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the little marks between the 12 and the 1.","explain":"There are 5 small marks from one number to the next, so each number is 5 minutes more."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Long hand on the 6, short hand between the 7 and the 8. What time is it?","choices":["half past 7","half past 8","6 o'clock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the hour the short hand has just passed.","explain":"The long hand on 6 is half past. The short hand has passed 7 but not reached 8, so it is half past 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Twenty to 3 in numbers is…","choices":["2:40","3:20","3:40"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twenty to 3 is 20 minutes before 3 o'clock, so it is still in the 2 o'clock hour.","explain":"20 minutes before 3:00 is 2:40, because 40 + 20 = 60."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"At quarter past, the long hand points to the…","choices":["3","15","9"],"answer":0,"hint":"There is no 15 on the clock face. Count in 5s to 15.","explain":"5, 10, 15 takes the long hand to the 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ella says the long hand on the 4 means 4 minutes past. What is her mistake?","choices":["each number is 5 minutes, so it is 20 past","nothing, it is 4 minutes past","it is 40 minutes past"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count in 5s from the 12 to the 4.","explain":"5, 10, 15, 20. The 4 stands for 20 minutes, so it is 20 past."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"At quarter to 6, where is the short hand?","choices":["nearly on the 6","right on the 6","on the 9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quarter to 6 is not quite 6 o'clock yet.","explain":"The short hand moves slowly. At quarter to 6 it is three quarters of the way from 5 to 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"The long hand points to the {n}. How many minutes to the next hour?","answer":"60-n*5","hint":"Count in 5s from the long hand on round to the 12.","explain":"{n} is {=n*5} minutes past, and 60 − {=n*5} = {=60-n*5} minutes to the next hour.","params":{"n":[7,11]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"School starts at 9:00. It is now {m} minutes past 8. How many minutes until school starts?","answer":"60-m","hint":"One hour is 60 minutes. Count on from the minutes now to 60.","explain":"60 − {m} = {=60-m} minutes until 9 o'clock.","params":{"m":[35,55]},"where":"m%5==0"}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The long hand points to the 3. How many minutes past the hour?","choices":["15","3","30"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count in 5s from the 12: 5, 10, …","explain":"5, 10, 15. The 3 means 3 jumps of 5, which is 15 minutes: quarter past."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"The long hand is on the 9 and the short hand is nearly on the 4. What time is it?","choices":["quarter to 4","quarter past 9","quarter to 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"The long hand on the 9 means 15 minutes to go. Which hour is coming next?","explain":"Long hand on 9 is quarter to. The short hand is nearly on 4, so it is quarter to 4, which is 3:45."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How do you write quarter past 2 in numbers?","choices":["2:15","2:25","3:15"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quarter past means 15 minutes after the hour.","explain":"Quarter past 2 is 15 minutes after 2 o'clock, so it is 2:15."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The long hand points to the {n}. How many minutes past the hour is that?","answer":"n*5","hint":"Each number is 5 minutes. Count in 5s from the 12 until you reach the number.","explain":"{n} jumps of 5 minutes is {=n*5} minutes past the hour.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"It is {m} minutes past 3. How many minutes until 4 o'clock?","answer":"60-m","hint":"An hour has 60 minutes. Take away the minutes that have gone.","explain":"60 − {m} = {=60-m}, so it is {=60-m} minutes to 4.","params":{"m":[5,55]},"where":"m%5==0"}],"explain":{"kid":["A clock has a long hand and a short hand. The long hand tells the minutes. The short hand tells the hour.","Count the long hand in 5s. Start at the 12 and say 5, 10, 15, 20 as you go round. If it points to the 4, it is 20 minutes past.","Long hand on the 3 is quarter past. Long hand on the 9 is quarter to. Quarter to 4 means 15 more minutes until 4 o'clock.","The short hand moves too, just slowly. At half past 7 it is in the middle, between the 7 and the 8."],"adult":["Year 2 maths in England asks children to tell and write the time to five minutes, including quarter past and quarter to the hour, and to draw the hands on a clock face to show these times. Year 1 covered o'clock and half past. In the US, first grade covers hours and half hours and second grade (2.MD.7) covers the nearest five minutes.","Words to use: minute hand (long hand), hour hand (short hand), minutes past, minutes to, quarter past, half past, quarter to, o'clock, and the digital form such as 3:45. Counting in 5s is the bridge: each numeral on the face stands for 5 minutes, and 12 jumps of 5 make 60 minutes, one hour.","Common mistakes: reading the number the long hand points to as the minutes (the 4 read as 4 minutes, not 20); swapping the hands; giving the wrong hour for 'to' times (calling 3:45 'quarter to 3' instead of 'quarter to 4'); and expecting the hour hand to sit exactly on a number all hour. Show that the short hand creeps forward as the long hand goes round.","In the lab your child counts round the clock in 5s, drags the minute hand to quarter past and quarter to, sees which side of the clock says 'past' and which says 'to', and watches the hour hand move with it."]},"familyTip":"Use a real clock or a paper plate clock with a split pin. Ask 'Where will the long hand be at quarter past?' before you turn it. At home, say times aloud: 'It is twenty past 5, tea in ten minutes.' Point to where the long hand will be when tea is ready.","keyIdea":"The long hand is the minute hand. From one number to the next is 5 minutes, so count the long hand in 5s from the 12: the 1 is 5 minutes past, the 3 is 15 (quarter past), the 6 is 30 (half past) and the 9 is 45 (quarter to the next hour). On the right of the clock we say minutes past the hour; on the left we say minutes to the next hour. The short hand moves slowly between the numbers, so at quarter to 4 it is nearly on the 4.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Past or to? Drag each time, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Minutes past","Minutes to"],"items":[["Long hand on 3",0],["Long hand on 9",1],["Long hand on 2",0],["Long hand on 8",1],["Quarter past 6",0],["Twenty to 4",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmins","caption":"Flagship lab: count the minute hand in 5s, drag it to quarter past and quarter to, and watch the hour hand creep to the next hour.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_KaF0SQvaiu":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Length is…","choices":["how long something is along a line","how loud it is","how heavy it is always"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think end to end in space.","explain":"Length measures distance along a line — not sound or mass."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A shifted start makes…","choices":["an unfair compare","a better measure","the object heavier"],"answer":0,"hint":"Starts must line up before any fair compare.","explain":"If starts do not line up, the compare is unfair. It does not change mass."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Height is about…","choices":["how tall something stands","only how wide it is","how colourful it is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Up from the ground or base.","explain":"Height is standing tall. Width and colour are different."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why use the same unit?","choices":["so the count means the same each time","so it looks pretty","so you can skip lining up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Units must be consistent.","explain":"Same-size units make counts comparable. Looks and skipping line-up are not the goal."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Tower A is {n} cubes tall. Tower B is {k} cubes tall. How many cubes taller is A?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract B’s height from A’s.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} cubes taller.","params":{"n":[6,9],"k":[2,5]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You need {n} cubes to measure a book. How many cubes do you need?","answer":"n","hint":"Use one cube per unit along the length.","explain":"You need {n} cubes for a fair end-to-end measure.","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Longer means…","choices":["covers more along the line","stands higher up always","has more colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Longer means more distance along a line.","explain":"Longer covers more end to end. Taller is height; colour is unrelated."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Mixing huge and tiny units in one count is…","choices":["unfair","the best method","required for height only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every unit in one measure must be the same size.","explain":"Mixed unit sizes make the number meaningless. Keep units the same."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Before comparing two lengths, you should…","choices":["line up the starting ends","only look at the far ends","flip one object over"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fair compares share the same start.","explain":"Line up the starts first. Comparing only far ends (or flipping) makes the compare unfair."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Taller means…","choices":["stands higher up","is always longer flat on the table","weighs more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Height is about how high something stands.","explain":"Taller is height. Longer is along a line. Weight is a different attribute."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A ribbon is {n} cubes long. Another is {k} cubes long. How much longer is the first?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the shorter cube count from the longer.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} cubes longer.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[2,4]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You place {n} cubes end to end. How many units long is the object?","answer":"n","hint":"Each cube is one unit when they touch with no gaps.","explain":"The object measures {n} cube units.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A fair cube measure needs…","choices":["cubes touching end to end","big random gaps","different sized units mixed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same unit, no gaps or overlaps.","explain":"Equal cubes must touch end to end. Gaps or mixed sizes make the count unfair."}],"explain":{"kid":["Length means how long something is from end to end. Height means how tall it stands.","Before you compare, line up the starting ends. Looking only at the far ends is not fair.","You can measure with cubes or hand spans. Use the same unit each time, touching end to end with no gaps.","Longer is not the same word as taller. A long ribbon can lie flat. A tall tower stands up."],"adult":["Year 1 measure: compare, describe and solve practical problems for lengths and heights; measure with non-standard units. Words: length, height, longer, shorter, taller, unit, line up, gap, overlap.","Common mistakes: comparing only far ends; leaving gaps between units; mixing unit sizes; swapping taller with longer; thinking heavier means longer.","In the lab your child lines up starts, picks longer vs shorter and taller vs shorter, lays cubes along a bar, fixes an unfair start/unit choice, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Put two pencils side by side. Ask: 'Did we line up the starts?' Then measure a book with cubes or paper clips: 'How many cubes long?'","keyIdea":"Length is how long something is along a line. Height is how tall it stands. To compare fairly, line up the starting ends. Measure with the same non-standard unit end to end — no gaps or overlaps.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fair measure or unfair? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair measure","Unfair measure"],"items":[["starts lined up, cubes end to end",0],["only comparing the far ends",1],["same-size cubes with no gaps",0],["big random gaps between units",1],["one start line for both objects",0],["mixing huge and tiny units in one count",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglenht","caption":"Flagship lab: line up starts, compare longer and taller, measure with cubes, and catch unfair-measure slips.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4lp_b5Pzik":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Holds more means…","choices":["takes more cups when full","is always taller","is half empty now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare how many equal cups each container takes when full.","explain":"Greater capacity means more cups when full."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A capacity record needs…","choices":["a number and a unit","only a doodle","the word empty alone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write both the number and the unit together, like 4 cups.","explain":"Write the number with its unit."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Litres are…","choices":["units for larger capacities","only used for length","the same as counting leaves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big bottles and jugs often show litres on the label.","explain":"Litres measure capacity for larger amounts — not length."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How full now is…","choices":["not the same as capacity","exactly the capacity always","why we skip measuring"],"answer":0,"hint":"A half-full snapshot is not how much the jug can hold.","explain":"A half-full jug still has the same capacity."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Jug A holds {n} cups. Jug B holds {k} cups. How many more cups does A hold?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract jug B’s cup count from jug A’s cup count.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} cups more.","params":{"n":[6,9],"k":[2,5]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You need {n} cups to fill a pot. How many cups is that?","answer":"n","hint":"The capacity in cups is how many you need when it is full.","explain":"The pot’s capacity is {n} cups.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why not trust height alone?","choices":["shape matters — measure with cups","taller always wins","cups never help"],"answer":0,"hint":"A tall thin jug can hold less — measure by counting equal cups.","explain":"Count equal cups; do not rank by height only."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Using unequal random cups is…","choices":["unfair","the best method","required for litres"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every pour must use the same cup size for a fair count.","explain":"Same cup size each pour. Mixed cups make the count unfair."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Capacity means…","choices":["how much it can hold when full","how full it is right now","only how tall it looks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the full amount the container can hold, not a snapshot.","explain":"Capacity is how much a container can hold when full — not how full it looks now."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"To measure with cups…","choices":["count equal cups that fill it","use random different cups","never pour anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the same cup size and pour until the jug is full.","explain":"Equal cups poured until full give a fair capacity measure."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A jug takes {n} cups. A bowl takes {k} cups. How many cups in all to fill both?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Add the jug capacity and the bowl capacity together.","explain":"{n} + {k} = {=n+k} cups to fill both.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A jug holds {n} cups. You have poured {k}. How many cups left to fill it?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the cups you have already poured from the full capacity.","explain":"Capacity {n}; poured {k}; {=n-k} cups left.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[1,4]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A tall thin jug…","choices":["might hold less than a short wide one","always holds the most","cannot be measured"],"answer":0,"hint":"Shape matters — count equal cups rather than trusting height alone.","explain":"Height alone misleads. Measure with equal cups."}],"explain":{"kid":["Capacity means how much a pot or jug can hold when it is full.","You can measure it by pouring the same cup again and again and counting the cups.","Write the answer with a number and a unit — like 4 cups. Big jugs may be about 1 litre.","A tall thin jug can hold less than a short wide one. How full it is right now is not its capacity."],"adult":["Year 1 Maths (measurement): measure and begin to record capacity/volume using non-standard units and litres. Distinct from qualitative full/empty language in 'Heavy and light, full and empty'. Words: capacity, volume, cup, litre, holds more, when full.","Common mistakes: equating capacity with how full something looks now; assuming taller always holds more; mixing unequal cups; recording a number with no unit.","In the lab your child fills by cup count, compares capacities, records cups and litres, rejects full-now snapshots, picks short-wide over tall-thin when cups say so, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Fill a jug using the same cup. Count: '1 cup, 2 cups…' Ask: 'Is the tall thin bottle really holding more than the short wide bowl?'","keyIdea":"Capacity is how much a container can hold when full. Measure it by counting equal cups that fill it, then record a number with a unit (cups, and litres for larger jugs). Taller does not always mean more; how full right now is not capacity.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Capacity idea or not? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["About capacity","Not capacity"],"items":[["count cups that fill the jug",0],["how full it looks this second",1],["write 4 cups as the measure",0],["taller always means more",1],["use litres for a big jug",0],["only guess from height",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcups","caption":"Flagship lab: fill by counting cups, compare who holds more, record units, separate full-now from capacity, and catch taller=more slips.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zd0YkB3xNj":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"On a balance, the heavier side…","choices":["goes down","floats up","cannot be compared"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gravity pulls the heavier pan lower.","explain":"The heavier pan goes down."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A mass record needs…","choices":["a number and a unit","only a doodle","the word empty alone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say how many and of what.","explain":"Write e.g. 4 cubes or 20 grams."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A big balloon compared with a small stone…","choices":["might be lighter — measure to check","is always heavier","cannot sit on a balance"],"answer":0,"hint":"Size is not the same as mass — check on a balance.","explain":"A big light object can lose to a small heavy one; measure to be sure."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Using random different-sized cubes…","choices":["makes the measure unfair","is the best method","is required by every scale"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every cube must be the same size so each unit means the same.","explain":"Unequal cubes break the unit; keep cubes identical."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A pointer on a cube scale points at {n}. What mass reading is that?","answer":"n","hint":"Read the number the pointer aims at.","explain":"Pointer at {n} means mass {n} cubes.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Object A balances {a} cubes. Object B balances {b} cubes. How many more cubes is B than A?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Subtract the smaller cube count from the larger.","explain":"B is {=b-a} cubes heavier than A.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[6,9]},"where":"b>a"},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Grams are…","choices":["standard units for mass","only used for length","the same as counting leaves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kitchen scales often mark mass in grams.","explain":"Grams are a standard unit for how heavy something is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why use a balance with cubes?","choices":["to measure mass in equal units","to make the object taller","to fill a cup with water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cubes are equal non-standard mass units.","explain":"Balancing equal cubes measures mass."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mass means…","choices":["how heavy something is","how tall something is","how full a cup looks now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the pull of weight, not height.","explain":"Mass is how heavy an object is."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"To measure mass with cubes…","choices":["add equal cubes until it balances","only look at how big it is","guess without a balance"],"answer":0,"hint":"Equal units and a level balance give the measure.","explain":"Add same-size cubes until both sides level, then count them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A toy balances {n} equal cubes. What mass do you record in cubes?","answer":"n","hint":"The cube count at balance is the mass in cube units.","explain":"Balanced with {n} cubes means the mass is {n} cubes.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A bag balances {n} cubes. You add {k} more identical cubes by mistake so it tips. How many cubes were the true mass?","answer":"n","hint":"The true mass is the count that first made both sides level.","explain":"True mass was {n} cubes; the extra {k} tipped it past balance.","params":{"n":[4,7],"k":[1,2]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Put the mass-measuring steps in order.","items":["place the object on one pan","add equal cubes on the other pan","stop when both sides are level","write the number of cubes with the unit"],"hint":"Object first, then cubes, then level, then record.","explain":"Balance first, then record number + unit."}],"explain":{"kid":["Mass means how heavy something is.","Put the thing on one side of a balance. Add the same size cubes on the other side until both sides are level.","Count the cubes. Write the number and the word cubes. That is the mass in cube units.","A big balloon can be lighter than a small stone. Measure — do not trust size alone."],"adult":["Year 1 measure: mass/weight with non-standard units, then begin standard units (grams). Words: mass, weight, balance, heavier, lighter, cubes, grams, unit, scale.","Common mistakes: thinking bigger always means heavier; recording a number with no unit; using unequal cubes; reading the wrong mark on a scale; confusing how full something looks with how heavy it is.","In the lab your child tips a balance, adds cubes until level, records 4 cubes and grams, picks the heavier stone over a big balloon, reads a pointer at 6, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Put a book on one side of a kitchen scale or homemade balance and add identical building cubes on the other until it balances. Ask: 'How many cubes is the book?' Then try a big empty box versus a small tin of beans.","keyIdea":"Mass is how heavy something is. Measure it by balancing equal cubes until both sides level, then record a number with a unit (for example 4 cubes). Simple scales can also show grams. Bigger is not always heavier — measure, do not guess from size alone.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fair mass measure or not? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair measure","Unfair / myth"],"items":[["add equal cubes until it balances",0],["only guess from how big it looks",1],["write 4 cubes as the mass",0],["bigger always means heavier",1],["read grams on a simple scale",0],["use random different-sized cubes",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmass","caption":"Flagship lab: tip a balance, add cubes until level, record number+unit, reject bigger=heavier, read a scale, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4m8BimI4G5":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"4:23 means…","choices":["23 minutes past 4","23 minutes to 4","quarter to 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Before :30, use minutes past.","explain":"4:23 is 23 minutes past 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Digital 2:09 matches…","choices":["9 minutes past 2","9 minutes to 2","half past 2"],"answer":0,"hint":":09 is still early in the hour.","explain":"2:09 is nine minutes past 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["Every time must land on a big number","3:17 is 17 minutes past 3","5:42 is 18 minutes to 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Small marks let you read every minute.","explain":"Times can land between big numbers; each mark is one minute."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Minute marks between numbers…","choices":["each mark is 1 minute","each mark is 5 minutes","only hours matter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big jumps are 5 minutes; small marks are 1.","explain":"Each small mark between numbers is one minute."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"6:51 as minutes to…","choices":["9 minutes to 7","51 minutes to 7","9 minutes past 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"60 − 51 = 9, next hour is 7.","explain":"6:51 is 9 minutes to 7 because 60 − 51 = 9, and the next hour is 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Minutes showing {a}. Minutes to next hour?","answer":"60-a","hint":"Subtract the minute reading from 60 to find minutes left.","explain":"60 − {a} = {=60-a} minutes to the next hour.","params":{"a":[40,58]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Start at 10 minutes past. Add {a} minutes. New minute reading?","answer":"10+a","hint":"Add on from 10; if you pass 60, wrap.","explain":"10 + {a} = {=10+a} minutes past the hour.","params":{"a":[1,20]}},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: set 3:17 on a clock.","items":["put the short hand near 3","move the long hand 17 marks","check digital 3:17"],"hint":"Hour hand near 3, minute hand on 17.","explain":"Set the hour area, count 17 minute marks, confirm digitally."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Three small marks past 12 means…","choices":["3 minutes past","15 minutes past","3 o'clock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each small mark is one minute, not five.","explain":"Three single marks past 12 is 3 minutes past."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"3:17 in words is…","choices":["17 minutes past 3","17 minutes to 3","quarter past 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Before half past, say minutes past.","explain":"3:17 is seventeen minutes past 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"5:42 as minutes to…","choices":["18 minutes to 6","42 minutes to 6","18 minutes past 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Minutes to = 60 minus the minute reading.","explain":"60 − 42 = 18, so 5:42 is 18 minutes to 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Minutes showing {a}. How many minutes to the next hour?","answer":"60-a","hint":"Subtract the minute reading from 60.","explain":"60 − {a} = {=60-a} minutes to the next hour.","params":{"a":[35,55]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Long hand on minute mark {a}. Minutes past?","answer":"a","hint":"Count one minute per small mark from 12.","explain":"Mark {a} past 12 means {a} minutes past.","params":{"a":[1,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Between the big numbers are small marks. Each small mark is one minute.","3:17 means 17 minutes past 3 — the long hand is not only on big numbers.","After half past you can say minutes to the next hour: 5:42 is 18 minutes to 6.","A digital clock and a clock face can show the same time in two ways."],"adult":["NC Y3 Measurement: tell and write the time from an analogue clock to the nearest minute; compare with digital. Builds on Y2 five-minute intervals (flagmins) and hours/half hours (flagclock).","Words: minute mark, minutes past, minutes to, digital, nearest minute. Common mistakes: reading only multiples of 5; swapping past and to; treating :09 as minutes to the hour.","In the lab your child counts seven marks to 3:07, sets 3:17 and 4:23, makes 5:42 as minutes to 6, matches 2:09, and spots the big-number-only mix-up."]},"familyTip":"Point to a wall clock and ask for the exact minute, not only 'about quarter past'. Say a digital time like 4:23 and have your child set the long hand.","keyIdea":"Every small mark on the clock is one minute. Read times like 3:17 and 5:42 — minutes past before half past, and minutes to the next hour after half past — and match the digital form.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Minute-time idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True minute idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["each small mark is 1 minute",0],["3:17 is 17 minutes past 3",0],["every time must land on a big number",1],["5:42 is 18 minutes to 6",0],["2:09 is 9 minutes to 2",1],["digital and analogue show the same time",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtomin","caption":"Flagship lab: count minute marks, set 3:17 and 4:23, read minutes to (5:42), match digital 2:09, catch multiples-of-5-only myth.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_DOe893F6gN":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Unit for how hot the day is?","choices":["°C","cm","litres"],"answer":0,"hint":"Heat and cold need a temperature unit.","explain":"Temperature uses degrees Celsius (°C)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["Always use cm for every length","Use m for a playground","Use ml for a medicine spoon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Long distances need larger length units.","explain":"Not every length should use cm — playgrounds need metres."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Juice carton capacity uses…","choices":["ml or litres","°C","kg only"],"answer":0,"hint":"A carton holds liquid — think capacity, not heat or mass.","explain":"Capacity of a carton is in ml or litres."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Paper width best unit?","choices":["cm","km","°C"],"answer":0,"hint":"A sheet of paper is short across.","explain":"Paper width is a small length — centimetres."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A grape’s mass is best in…","choices":["g","kg","m"],"answer":0,"hint":"A grape is light; pick the small mass unit, not kilograms.","explain":"A grape is about grams, not kilograms or metres."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"How many cm in {a} metres?","answer":"a*100","hint":"1 m = 100 cm. Multiply metres by 100.","explain":"{a} metres equals {=a*100} centimetres.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{a} litres as 100 ml cups (10 per litre)?","answer":"a*10","hint":"Multiply litres by 10 for 100 ml cups.","explain":"{a} litres is about {=a*10} cups of 100 ml.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Choosing units means…","choices":["matching unit to length, mass, heat or capacity","always using cm","using kg for temperature"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think which attribute you are measuring.","explain":"Pick m/cm, kg/g, °C, or ml/litres to match the job."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Best unit for a pencil’s length?","choices":["cm","m","kg"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pencil is short — think small length units.","explain":"A pencil is measured in centimetres, not metres or kilograms."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Silly unit for a swimming pool’s capacity?","choices":["millilitres (ml)","metres for length","large capacity units"],"answer":0,"hint":"Millilitres are tiny drops.","explain":"Millilitres are far too small for a pool’s capacity."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Best unit for a full school bag’s mass?","choices":["kg","g","°C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Heavy to lift — grams or kilograms?","explain":"A full bag is about kilograms, not grams or degrees."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"How many centimetres are in {a} metres?","answer":"a*100","hint":"1 metre = 100 centimetres. Multiply the metres by 100.","explain":"{a} metres equals {=a*100} centimetres — useful when you switch length units.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A jug holds {a} litres. About how many 100 ml cups is that (10 cups per litre × litres)?","answer":"a*10","hint":"Each litre is about ten 100 ml cups.","explain":"{a} litres × 10 cups per litre ≈ {=a*10} cups.","params":{"a":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Different jobs need different units.","Length uses centimetres or metres. Mass uses grams or kilograms.","Temperature uses degrees Celsius. Capacity uses millilitres or litres.","A tiny unit for a huge thing — or the wrong kind of unit — is a mix-up."],"adult":["NC Y2 Measurement: choose and use the right units — m or cm for length, kg or g for mass, °C for temperature, litres or ml for capacity. Distinct from Measuring Length and Height, Measuring mass and weight, and reading a thermometer.","Words: unit, centimetre, metre, gram, kilogram, Celsius, millilitre, litre. Common mistakes: always using cm; using kg for heat; using ml for a swimming pool.","In the lab your child matches object to unit, spots a silly unit, estimates then picks, chooses °C, catches the always-cm myth, and sorts capacity units."]},"familyTip":"Point at a pencil, a door, a juice carton and a bag. Ask: cm or m? ml or litres? g or kg?","keyIdea":"Pick the unit that fits what you measure: cm or m for length, g or kg for mass, °C for temperature, ml or litres for capacity — not one unit for everything.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sensible unit idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True unit idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["pencil length → cm",0],["playground length → m",0],["always use cm for every length",1],["day’s heat → °C",0],["full school bag → kg",0],["swimming pool → millilitres",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagunits","caption":"Flagship lab: match pencil→cm, spot silly pool units, estimate bag→kg, pick °C, catch always-cm myth, sort capacity units.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_BFJ-ch_8QU":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Put in order, shortest first: 15 cm, 9 cm, 22 cm","choices":["9 cm, 15 cm, 22 cm","15 cm, 9 cm, 22 cm","22 cm, 15 cm, 9 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the smallest number first.","explain":"9 is the smallest and 22 is the biggest: 9 cm < 15 cm < 22 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A tall jug and a wide jug both fill a measuring jug to 500 ml. Which symbol fits: tall ? wide","choices":["=",">","<"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the measurements, not the shapes.","explain":"Both hold 500 ml, so they hold the same: 500 ml = 500 ml."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which symbol fits: 3 kg ? 5 kg","choices":["<",">","="],"answer":0,"hint":"Which mass is less? The sign points to the smaller one.","explain":"3 is less than 5, so 3 kg < 5 kg."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam says 90 cm is longer than 1 m because 90 is more than 1. What went wrong?","choices":["He compared different units","He used the wrong sign only","Nothing, he is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is 1 m in centimetres?","explain":"1 m is 100 cm. Compare 100 cm with 90 cm: 1 m is longer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A ribbon is {a} cm long. A string is {b} cm long. How much longer is the ribbon in cm?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the shorter length from the longer one.","explain":"{a} cm take away {b} cm is {=a-b} cm.","params":{"a":[30,50],"b":[10,25]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A pencil starts at 2 and ends at 10 on a ruler. Which is true?","choices":["The pencil < 10 cm","The pencil = 10 cm","The pencil > 10 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"It did not start at 0. Count the gap from 2 to 10.","explain":"From 2 to 10 is 8 cm, and 8 cm < 10 cm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Parcel A weighs {a} g. Parcel B weighs {b} g. How many grams heavier is parcel B?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Parcel B has more grams. Take A's grams from B's grams.","explain":"{b} g take away {a} g is {=b-a} g, so {a} g < {b} g and B is {=b-a} g heavier.","params":{"a":[100,180],"b":[250,450]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which symbol fits: 60 cm ? 6 cm","choices":[">","<","="],"answer":0,"hint":"60 and 6 share a digit, but which is more?","explain":"60 is ten times 6, so 60 cm > 6 cm."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which symbol fits: 45 cm ? 32 cm","choices":[">","<","="],"answer":0,"hint":"Which length is more? The open side of the sign faces the bigger one.","explain":"45 is more than 32, so 45 cm > 32 cm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A crayon starts at {s} cm and ends at {e} cm on a ruler. How long is the crayon in cm?","answer":"e-s","hint":"It does not start at 0. Find the gap from the start to the end.","explain":"From {s} to {e} is {=e-s}, so the crayon is {=e-s} cm long.","params":{"s":[1,5],"e":[10,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A big box weighs 150 g. A small tin weighs 400 g. Which is heavier?","choices":["The small tin","The big box","They weigh the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the grams, not the sizes.","explain":"400 g is more than 150 g, so the small tin is heavier: 150 g < 400 g."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Jug A holds {a} ml. Jug B holds {b} ml. How many more ml does jug A hold?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Find the difference: take the smaller amount from the bigger one.","explain":"{a} ml take away {b} ml is {=a-b} ml, so jug A holds {=a-b} ml more.","params":{"a":[60,90],"b":[20,50]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is longer: 1 m or 90 cm?","choices":["1 m","90 cm","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change 1 m into centimetres first.","explain":"1 m is 100 cm, and 100 cm is more than 90 cm, so 1 m > 90 cm."}],"explain":{"kid":["To compare two things, measure them both. Then look at the numbers and the units. Put the start of the thing at 0 on the ruler. Then read the number at the end. If it starts at 4 and ends at 16, it is 12 cm, not 16 cm.","Write what you find with a sign. 45 cm > 32 cm means 45 cm is more. 150 g < 400 g means 150 g is less. 500 ml = 500 ml means they are the same.","Big does not always mean heavy. A big box of popcorn can be lighter than a small tin. A tall jug can hold the same as a wide jug. Measure to find out.","Use the same unit for both. 1 m is 100 cm, so 1 m is more than 90 cm. To put three in order, start with the smallest number."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. National Curriculum in England, Year 2 measurement: compare and order lengths, mass, volume and capacity, and record the results using >, < and =. Children measure in standard units (cm and m, g and kg, ml and litres) first.","A comparison is only fair when both measurements are taken correctly and are in the same unit. Length is read from a 0 start, or found as the difference between start and end. Mass and capacity are read from scales. The symbols then record the comparison of the two measurements.","Common mistakes: reading only the end mark when the object does not start at 0; judging mass by size (bigger means heavier) or capacity by height (taller means holds more) instead of measuring; comparing numbers in different units (thinking 90 cm is longer than 1 m because 90 > 1); and turning the < or > sign the wrong way.","In the lab your child slides a ribbon to the 0 mark, records 45 cm > 32 cm, weighs a big box and a small tin, pours a tall jug and a wide jug into a measuring jug, orders three strings that do not start in line, and changes 1 m into 100 cm before comparing it with 90 cm."]},"familyTip":"Measure three things at home, such as a spoon, a sock and a book, in centimetres. Write the results with < or >, then put them in order. Weigh a big light thing and a small heavy thing on kitchen scales and ask which is heavier before you look.","keyIdea":"Measure first, then compare the numbers with their units. Start a ruler at 0, read scales carefully, and use the same unit for both things. Record the result with > (greater than), < (less than) or = (the same), and order several measurements from smallest to greatest.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which symbol fits each pair of measurements? Drag each card into >, = or <, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":[">","=","<"],"items":[["45 cm ? 32 cm",0],["150 g ? 400 g",2],["500 ml ? 500 ml",1],["1 m ? 90 cm",0],["3 kg ? 5 kg",2],["20 cm ? 20 cm",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcmpm","caption":"Flagship lab: slide a ribbon to 0 on a ruler, weigh a box and a tin, pour two jugs, order three strings and compare 1 m with 90 cm.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Jvvh5P06NV":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A room is 5 m long and 4 m wide. What is its area?","choices":["20 m²","9 m²","18 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tile it with 1 metre squares in your head: 4 rows of 5.","explain":"4 rows of 5 metre squares is 5 × 4 = 20 m². 18 m is the perimeter, a length round the edge."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which rectangle has an area of 12 square units?","choices":["3 by 4","3 by 9","5 by 7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply the two sides of each rectangle.","explain":"3 × 4 = 12. The others are 3 × 9 = 27 and 5 × 7 = 35."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A chocolate bar has {r} rows with {k} squares in each row. How many squares are there?","answer":"r*k","hint":"Equal rows: multiply the number of rows by the squares in one row.","explain":"{r} rows of {k} is {r} × {k} = {=r*k} squares.","params":{"r":[2,5],"k":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A 4 by 3 rectangle has an area of 12. It is made 8 long and still 3 wide. What is the new area?","choices":["24","16","12"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each row now has 8 squares. How many rows are there?","explain":"3 rows of 8 is 8 × 3 = 24, double the old area of 12."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why is the area of a 6 by 4 rectangle the same as 6 × 4?","choices":["It has 4 equal rows of 6 squares","Because 6 + 4 is 10","Because it has 4 sides"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture the squares in rows. Are the rows the same?","explain":"Tiled, it makes 4 rows with 6 squares in each. Equal rows of 6 is 4 lots of 6, which is 6 × 4 = 24."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A rectangle is 7 cm long and 2 cm wide. Which answer has the right number and unit?","choices":["14 cm²","14 cm","9 cm²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply the sides, then remember area is counted in squares.","explain":"7 × 2 = 14 squares of 1 cm², so the area is 14 cm², not 14 cm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A patio is {a} m long and {b} m wide. It is covered with 1 metre square slabs. How many slabs are needed?","answer":"a*b","hint":"There are {b} rows of slabs with {a} slabs in each row.","explain":"{b} rows of {a} slabs is {a} × {b} = {=a*b} slabs, an area of {=a*b} m².","params":{"a":[4,9],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A rectangle has an area of 18 square units. One side is 6. How long is the other side?","choices":["3","12","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many rows of 6 make 18?","explain":"3 rows of 6 is 18, so the other side is 3, because 6 × 3 = 18."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A rectangle is tiled with 3 rows. Each row has 5 unit squares. What is its area?","choices":["15 square units","8 square units","16 square units"],"answer":0,"hint":"3 rows of 5. Multiply the rows by the squares in each row.","explain":"3 rows of 5 is 5, 10, 15, so the area is 3 × 5 = 15 square units."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A rectangle is {a} cm long and {b} cm wide. It is tiled with 1 cm squares. What is its area in cm²?","answer":"a*b","hint":"There are {b} rows, and each row has {a} squares. Multiply.","explain":"{b} rows of {a} squares is {a} × {b} = {=a*b}, so the area is {=a*b} cm².","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Only the top row and the first column of a rectangle are tiled. The top row has 8 squares. The column has 4 squares. What is the area?","choices":["32 square units","12 square units","11 square units"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every row is the same as the top row. How many rows are there?","explain":"There are 4 rows of 8, so the area is 4 × 8 = 32 square units. 12 adds the sides and 11 counts only the drawn squares."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A rug has an area of {a} × {b} square metres. It is turned round, so it is {b} m long and {a} m wide. What is its area now in m²?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Turning a rectangle round does not change how many squares cover it.","explain":"{b} × {a} is the same as {a} × {b}, so the area is still {=a*b} m².","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A rectangle is 6 cm long and 4 cm wide. Mia says its area is 10 cm². What did she do?","choices":["She added the sides instead of multiplying","She found the perimeter","Nothing, she is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out 6 + 4 and 6 × 4. Which is the number of squares inside?","explain":"6 + 4 = 10 adds the sides. The area is 4 rows of 6 squares: 6 × 4 = 24 cm²."}],"explain":{"kid":["Area is how many unit squares cover a shape. To find the area of a rectangle, tile it with squares in rows. A rectangle 6 squares long and 4 squares wide has 4 rows. Each row has 6 squares.","You can count the rows in steps: 6, 12, 18, 24. That is 4 lots of 6, so 4 × 6 = 24. The area is the length times the width.","You do not need to draw every square. If you know one row has 7 squares and there are 3 rows, the area is 3 × 7 = 21. Do not add the sides: 7 + 3 is not the number of squares inside.","Turn a rectangle round and the area stays the same, because 3 × 5 = 5 × 3. Write the unit as squares: cm² for square centimetres and m² for square metres."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. This matches the US Common Core standard 3.MD.C.7a: find the area of a rectangle with whole-number side lengths by tiling it, and show that the answer equals the product of the side lengths. In England, Year 4 finds the area of rectilinear shapes by counting squares, which leads to the length × width formula in Year 5.","Tiling turns a rectangle into an array. The rows are equal groups, so the area is rows × squares in a row, which is width × length. This is why the formula works, and it links area to multiplication facts and to the commutative law: a 3 by 5 rectangle turned round is a 5 by 3 rectangle with the same 15 squares.","Common mistakes: adding the two side lengths (6 + 4 = 10) instead of multiplying; counting the squares round the edge or finding the perimeter (20) instead of the area; counting only the squares that are drawn when a diagram shows just one row and one column; and writing the area in cm or m instead of square units (cm² or m²).","In the lab your child lays rows of 6 into a 6 by 4 rectangle and sees 6, 12, 18, 24 become 4 × 6, picks 3 × 7 from a part-tiled rectangle, turns 5 by 3 into 3 by 5, stretches a rectangle to find 3 × 4 and 2 × 6, doubles one side to double the area, and tiles a 4 m by 3 m room to find 12 m²."]},"familyTip":"Find a rectangle at home that is covered in squares, such as a chocolate bar, an ice cube tray or floor tiles. Count the squares in one row and the number of rows, then multiply. Count them one by one to check. Ask which is quicker.","keyIdea":"Tile a rectangle with unit squares in rows. Every row has the same number of squares, so the area is the number of rows times the number in each row. That is the same as multiplying the two side lengths: a 4 by 6 rectangle has 4 × 6 = 24 square units. Area is measured in squares, such as cm² or m².","visual":{"type":"array","caption":"An array of unit squares: rows of the same length. Count the rows and the squares in one row, then multiply.","opts":{"rows":4,"cols":6}},"lab":{"type":"flagtiles","caption":"Flagship lab: lay rows of squares, multiply from one row and column, turn a rectangle, find two with area 12, double a side, tile a room in m².","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_6oxQPNLHNv":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many grams are in 1 kg?","choices":["1000 g","100 g","10 g"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kilo means a thousand, as in kilometre.","explain":"1 kg = 1000 g. So 1 kg − 300 g = 700 g."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A scale counts up in 50 g. The needle is one mark after 600 g. What is the mass?","choices":["650 g","601 g","700 g"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each small mark is worth 50 g here, not 1 g.","explain":"One 50 g mark after 600 g is 650 g."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A plant was {a} cm tall. Now it is {b} cm tall. How many cm did it grow?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Find the difference: count on from {a} to {b}.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a}, so it grew {=b-a} cm.","params":{"a":[12,29],"b":[35,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is 2 m 15 cm in centimetres?","choices":["215 cm","17 cm","2015 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each metre is 100 cm, so change the metres first.","explain":"2 m is 200 cm. 200 cm + 15 cm = 215 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"47 cm + 36 cm. Which answer is right?","choices":["83 cm","73 cm","11 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"7 + 6 = 13 ones. That is 1 ten and 3 ones.","explain":"40 + 30 = 70, and 7 + 6 = 13. 70 + 13 = 83 cm. 73 forgets the new ten."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A bottle holds 1 litre. You drink 250 ml two times. How much is left?","choices":["500 ml","750 ml","998 ml"],"answer":0,"hint":"1 litre is 1000 ml. Two drinks of 250 ml are 500 ml.","explain":"1000 ml − 500 ml = 500 ml, half a litre."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"How many millimetres are in {c} cm?","answer":"10*c","hint":"Each centimetre has 10 millimetres.","explain":"{c} × 10 = {=10*c}, so {c} cm = {=10*c} mm.","params":{"c":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia adds 1 kg and 200 g and writes 201 g. What should she have done first?","choices":["Change 1 kg to 1000 g, then add to get 1200 g","Nothing, 201 g is right","Take 200 from 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"You can only add masses in the same unit.","explain":"1 kg = 1000 g, and 1000 g + 200 g = 1200 g."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A pencil ends 6 small marks after 9 cm on a ruler. How long is it?","choices":["96 mm","15 mm","9.6 mm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each cm is 10 mm, so 9 cm is 90 mm. Add the small marks.","explain":"9 cm is 90 mm, and 6 more mm makes 96 mm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A ribbon is {a} cm long. Another is {b} cm long. How long are they end to end, in cm?","answer":"a+b","hint":"End to end means add the two lengths. Watch for a new ten from the ones.","explain":"{a} cm + {b} cm = {=a+b} cm.","params":{"a":[25,58],"b":[17,39]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A jug holds 1 litre. You pour out {p} ml. How many ml are left?","answer":"1000-p","hint":"1 litre is 1000 ml. Take away what you poured.","explain":"1000 ml − {p} ml = {=1000-p} ml left.","params":{"p":[100,900]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ali says 1 m + 40 cm = 41 cm. What went wrong?","choices":["He treated 1 m as 1 cm; it is 100 cm, so the answer is 140 cm","Nothing, 41 cm is right","He should have got 50 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many centimetres are in 1 metre?","explain":"1 m = 100 cm, so 1 m + 40 cm = 140 cm, or 1 m 40 cm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A bag of rice has a mass of {r} g. A bag of pasta has a mass of 500 g. What is the total mass in grams?","answer":"r+500","hint":"Both are in grams already, so add the numbers.","explain":"{r} g + 500 g = {=r+500} g.","params":{"r":[150,450]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A ruler shows centimetres. Each centimetre has 10 tiny marks called millimetres. So 1 cm = 10 mm, and 7 cm and 4 mm is 74 mm.","To add two lengths, put them end to end. 45 cm and 38 cm make 83 cm. The 5 and 8 make 13, so you get a new ten.","You can only add things in the same unit. 1 m is 100 cm. So 1 m 30 cm is 130 cm. Add 85 cm and you get 215 cm.","Grams and millilitres work the same way. 1 kg = 1000 g and 1 litre = 1000 ml. Pour 250 ml two times from 1 litre and 500 ml is left."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. In England, Year 3 pupils measure, compare, add and subtract lengths in m, cm and mm, mass in kg and g, and capacity in l and ml. This builds on Year 2, where they chose units and compared measures with <, > and =.","Two rules carry most of the work. First, convert to one unit, using 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 kg = 1000 g and 1 l = 1000 ml. Second, use the usual written or mental methods and keep the unit on the answer. A difference, such as how much a plant grew, can be found by counting on from the smaller value.","Common mistakes: adding mixed units as if they were the same (1 m + 30 cm written as 31). Thinking 1 kg is 100 g. Reading every small mark on a scale as 1 when each one is worth 50. Forgetting the new ten made by the ones. Adding the two heights when the question asks how much more.","In the lab your child reads three lengths to the millimetre. They drag one ribbon to meet another and add the lengths. They change 1 m 30 cm to 130 cm before adding. They read a scale that counts in 50 g, pour two 250 ml glasses from a 1 litre jug, and hop along a number line to find how much a plant grew."]},"familyTip":"Measure two things with a ruler or tape and ask how long they would be end to end. Use a kitchen scale: weigh one item, add another and ask your child to say the new mass before reading the scale. When you pour a drink from a 1 litre carton, ask how much is left.","keyIdea":"Before you add or subtract measurements, put them in the same unit: 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 kg = 1000 g and 1 l = 1000 ml. Then calculate with the numbers and keep the unit on the answer. To find how much longer, heavier or more, find the difference.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Add, or take away to find what is left or the difference? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Add","Take away or find the difference"],"items":[["Two ribbons of 45 cm and 38 cm joined",0],["A 1 litre jug after pouring out 250 ml",1],["A plant that grew from 23 cm to 41 cm",1],["A 350 g bag and a 400 g bag on a scale",0],["A 2 m rope with 60 cm cut off",1],["A walk of 300 m and then 450 m more",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmcalc","caption":"Flagship lab: read mm, join ribbons, change 1 m 30 cm to cm before adding, read a 50 g scale, pour from 1 litre, count on to find growth.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ML5t7n2-U8":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Some cloth is cut into a triangle with a base of {=2*k} cm and a perpendicular height of {h} cm. What is its area in cm²?","answer":"k*h","hint":"Work out base × height, then halve it: a triangle is half a parallelogram.","explain":"½ × {=2*k} × {h} = {=k*h} cm².","params":{"k":[2,6],"h":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A parallelogram-shaped tile has a base of {b} cm and a perpendicular height of {h} cm. What is its area in cm²?","answer":"b*h","hint":"Do not halve for a parallelogram: it slides into a whole rectangle.","explain":"Area = base × height = {b} × {h} = {=b*h} cm². No halving, because a parallelogram rearranges into a whole rectangle.","params":{"b":[4,12],"h":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A triangle has a base of 10 cm, a slanted side of 5 cm and a perpendicular height of 4 cm. What is its area?","choices":["20 cm²","25 cm²","40 cm²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the height that is at a right angle to the base, and remember to halve.","explain":"½ × 10 × 4 = 20 cm². 25 cm² uses the slanted side, and 40 cm² forgets to halve."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You push the top of a rectangle sideways to make a parallelogram. The base and the height stay the same. What happens to the area?","choices":["It stays the same","It gets bigger, because the sides get longer","It gets smaller, because the shape is squashed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Area depends on the base and the perpendicular height.","explain":"The sides get longer, so the perimeter grows, but base × height has not changed, so the area is the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A triangle and a parallelogram have the same base and the same perpendicular height. Which is true?","choices":["The triangle's area is half the parallelogram's","They have equal areas","The triangle's area is a third of the parallelogram's"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many copies of the triangle fill the parallelogram?","explain":"Two copies of the triangle make the parallelogram, so the triangle's area is half."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A triangle has an area of {=k*h} cm² and a perpendicular height of {h} cm. What is its base in cm?","answer":"2*k","hint":"Double the area to get base × height, then divide by the height.","explain":"{=k*h} × 2 = {=2*k*h}, and {=2*k*h} ÷ {h} = {=2*k}. The base is {=2*k} cm.","params":{"k":[2,6],"h":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which unit could measure the area of a triangle?","choices":["cm²","cm","cm³"],"answer":0,"hint":"Area counts how many unit squares cover the shape.","explain":"Area counts square units, so it is measured in cm² (or m², mm²). cm is a length and cm³ is a volume."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In an obtuse triangle the top corner is past the end of the base. Where is the perpendicular height?","choices":["Outside the triangle, down to the base line extended","Along the longest side","There is no height"],"answer":0,"hint":"The height always meets the base line at a right angle.","explain":"Extend the base line and drop the height from the top corner at a right angle. It lies outside the triangle but is still the height."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A garden bed is a parallelogram {b} m long with a perpendicular width of {h} m. A bag of bark covers 1 m². How many bags are needed?","answer":"b*h","hint":"Work out the area in m², one bag per square metre.","explain":"{b} × {h} = {=b*h} m², so {=b*h} bags.","params":{"b":[3,9],"h":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A parallelogram has a base of {b} cm and a perpendicular height of {h} cm. What is its area in cm²?","answer":"b*h","hint":"A parallelogram rearranges into a rectangle with the same base and height.","explain":"Area = base × height = {b} × {h} = {=b*h} cm².","params":{"b":[3,12],"h":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A triangle has a base of {=2*k} cm and a perpendicular height of {h} cm. What is its area in cm²?","answer":"k*h","hint":"Work out base × height, then halve it, because a triangle is half a parallelogram.","explain":"½ × {=2*k} × {h} = {=k*h} cm². Base × height is {=2*k*h}, and half of that is {=k*h}.","params":{"k":[2,7],"h":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A parallelogram has a base of 6 cm, slanted sides of 5 cm and a perpendicular height of 4 cm. Which calculation gives its area?","choices":["6 × 4","6 × 5","6 × 5 ÷ 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which length meets the base at a right angle?","explain":"The height is 4 cm because it is perpendicular to the base. 6 × 4 = 24 cm². The 5 cm side leans, so it is longer than the height."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why is the area of a triangle half of base × height?","choices":["Two copies of the triangle fit together to make a parallelogram with that base and height","A triangle has three sides instead of four","Triangles are always half as tall as rectangles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the turned copy in the lab.","explain":"Turn a copy of the triangle and join it on: the pair makes a parallelogram of area base × height, so one triangle is half."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A parallelogram has an area of {=b*h} cm² and a base of {b} cm. What is its perpendicular height in cm?","answer":"h","hint":"Base × height = area, so divide the area by the base.","explain":"{=b*h} ÷ {b} = {h}, so the height is {h} cm.","params":{"b":[3,9],"h":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A parallelogram is a four-sided shape with two pairs of parallel sides. It looks like a rectangle that has been pushed over. Cut a triangle off one end, slide it to the other end, and you get a rectangle. Nothing is added and nothing is lost, so the area stays the same.","That rectangle is as long as the base of the parallelogram and as tall as its height. So the area of a parallelogram is base × height. A base of 8 cm and a height of 5 cm gives 8 × 5 = 40 cm². Area is always in square units, like cm². To work back, height = area ÷ base.","The height is the distance straight up from the base. It meets the base at a right angle. The slanted side is not the height. It leans over, so it is longer. If you use it, your answer comes out too big.","Now take any triangle and an exact copy. Turn the copy upside down and join it on. The two triangles make a parallelogram. So one triangle is half of it: area = ½ × base × height. A base of 12 cm and a height of 7 cm gives ½ × 12 × 7 = 42 cm²."],"adult":["Ages 10 to 11 (NC Year 6: calculate the area of parallelograms and triangles; CCSS 6.G.A.1). The two formulae are not new rules to learn by heart. Both come from the rectangle. A parallelogram can be cut and moved into a rectangle with the same base and height. Two copies of a triangle make a parallelogram.","Any side can be the base. The height that goes with it is the distance from that base to the opposite side or corner, measured at a right angle. In an obtuse triangle this height can fall outside the shape, on the base line made longer. The lab shows this when the top corner slides past the end of the base.","Common mistakes: using the slanted side instead of the height; forgetting to halve for a triangle, or halving a parallelogram; adding base and height; and writing cm instead of cm². Many children also think that leaning a shape over changes its area, because the sides get longer. The perimeter grows, but the area stays the same.","In the lab your child slides a cut triangle to make a rectangle, leans the top edge and sees the area stay at 40 cm², picks between a 5 cm slanted side and a 4 cm height, joins a turned copy of a triangle into a parallelogram, drags a top corner past the base, and builds a triangle of 18 cm²."]},"familyTip":"Cut a parallelogram out of squared paper. Ask your child to cut it once and rearrange it into a rectangle, then count the squares in both. Next cut a parallelogram along a diagonal: do the two triangles match exactly? Ask which length they would measure with a ruler and set square to find the height.","keyIdea":"A parallelogram can be cut and rearranged into a rectangle with the same base and the same height, so its area is base × height. Two copies of any triangle fit together to make a parallelogram, so a triangle's area is half of that: ½ × base × height. The height must be the perpendicular height, measured at a right angle to the base, not the length of a slanted side.","visual":{"type":"formula","caption":"Slide the base and the height. Triangle area = base × height ÷ 2, half of the parallelogram with the same base and height.","opts":{"name":"triangle area","expr":"b × h ÷ 2","vars":{"b":[2,12,8],"h":[1,10,5]},"unit":"cm²"}},"lab":{"type":"flagtripar","caption":"Flagship lab: slide a triangle to make a rectangle, lean a parallelogram, find the height, join two triangles, build 18 cm².","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_skYly2Qm01":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The pen is longer than the crayon. The crayon is longer than the chalk. Which is shortest?","choices":["The chalk","The pen","The crayon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the one that loses both times.","explain":"The crayon beats the chalk, and the pen beats the crayon. So the chalk is the shortest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Mia is taller than Sam. Sam is taller than Jo. Is Mia taller than Jo?","choices":["Yes","No","We would have to check"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sam is in the middle. Who beats Sam, and who does Sam beat?","explain":"Mia beats Sam, and Sam beats Jo. So Mia beats Jo too. Sam is the go-between."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A stick is shorter than the table and shorter than the rug. Does the stick show which is longer?","choices":["No, both are longer than the stick","Yes, the table","Yes, the rug"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the stick reach the end of either one?","explain":"Both are longer than the stick, so it cannot decide. Use a string as long as one of them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the first thing to do when you compare two ribbons?","choices":["Line up the starts","Look at the colours","Fold them in half"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where should both ribbons begin?","explain":"Line up the starting ends. Then the ribbon whose other end sticks out is longer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your string is as long as the door. It is shorter than the window. What does that tell you?","choices":["The window is longer than the door","The door is longer than the window","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"The string is the door's length.","explain":"The string matches the door. The window is longer than the string, so the window is longer than the door."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A string is {a} blocks long. The gap is {b} blocks. How many blocks of string are left over?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the gap away from the string.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} blocks left over. The string is longer than the gap.","params":{"a":[9,12],"b":[5,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ribbons are 3, 8 and 5 blocks. Which order is shortest to longest?","choices":["3, 5, 8","3, 8, 5","8, 5, 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with the smallest number.","explain":"3 is shortest, then 5, then 8. That is shortest to longest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Tom's tower is {a} cubes tall. Pia's is {b} cubes taller. How tall is Pia's?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Pia has more cubes. Add the extra cubes to Tom's.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} cubes. Pia's tower is taller.","params":{"a":[4,8],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why use a string and not just look?","choices":["Looking can trick you; the string carries the real length","Strings are pretty","Looking is always right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can your eyes judge things far apart?","explain":"Things far apart are hard to judge by eye. A tight string carries the real length."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Red ribbon starts further left than blue. Blue's end sticks out more. Which is longer?","choices":["Line up the starts, then check","Blue, because its end sticks out","Red, because it starts first"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are the starts in the same place?","explain":"When the starts are not lined up, the ends can trick you. Move both to the same start line, then compare the other ends."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A string is {a} blocks long. A sofa is {b} blocks long. How many blocks longer is the string?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the sofa's length away from the string's length.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}. The string is {=a-b} blocks longer, so the gap it measured is longer than the sofa.","params":{"a":[8,10],"b":[4,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You cannot move the bed or the door. How can you compare their lengths?","choices":["Use a string as a go-between","Guess by looking","You cannot compare them"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could carry a length from one place to another?","explain":"Make a string as long as the bed. Then hold it against the door. The string carries the bed's length across the room."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ribbons are {a}, {=a+2} and {=a+5} blocks long. How long is the longest one?","answer":"a+5","hint":"Line them up. Which number is biggest?","explain":"The biggest number is the longest ribbon: {=a+5} blocks.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Your string is curly and loose when you measure. What happens?","choices":["It says the thing is longer than it is","It gives the right length","It says the thing is shorter"],"answer":0,"hint":"A curl uses up some string.","explain":"Curls use up string, so when you lay it flat it is longer than the thing. Pull it tight and straight."}],"explain":{"kid":["To put three ribbons in order, first line up their starts. Then look at the other ends. The one that sticks out most is the longest.","Some things can not move, like a bed or a door. You can still compare them. Lay a string along the bed and pinch it at the end. Now take the string to the door. The string is a go-between.","Pull the string tight. A wavy string is longer than it looks, so it gives a wrong answer.","If Ali is taller than Ben, and Ben is taller than Cat, then Ali is taller than Cat. You do not need to stand Ali and Cat together."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. This is Common Core grade 1 measurement, 1.MD.1: order three objects by length, and compare the lengths of two objects indirectly by using a third object. Key words: length, longer, shorter, longest, shortest, compare, order, start line, go-between.","Indirect comparison rests on one rule. If A is longer than B and B is longer than C, then A is longer than C. Mathematicians call this transitivity. The third object carries a length from one place to another. Children who grasp it are ready for rulers, which are just go-betweens with numbers on them.","Common mistakes: judging the longest by whichever end reaches furthest, when the starts do not line up; letting the string sag or curl, which measures too long; and picking a go-between shorter than both things, which cannot decide. Some children also flip the order and call the shortest item the longest.","In the lab your child lines up three ribbons. They carry a string from a gap to a sofa, and pull a wavy string tight. They compare Ali, Ben and Cat two at a time to find the tallest. Then they see that a short stick cannot decide, but a string can."]},"familyTip":"Cut a piece of string as long as the sofa. Before you test it, ask your child: is the bed longer or shorter than this string? Then carry the string to the bed and check. Swap jobs: let them pick two things that cannot move, like the fridge and a door, and you guess.","keyIdea":"To order three things by length, line up their starting ends and compare the other ends. When two things cannot be put side by side, use a third thing, like a piece of string, as a go-between: make it as long as one thing, then hold it against the other. Keep the string tight and straight. If A is longer than B, and B is longer than C, then A is longer than C.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can you put these two side by side, or do you need a go-between? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Side by side","Need a go-between"],"items":[["two pencils",0],["the door and the bed",1],["two ribbons",0],["the fridge and the window",1],["two toy cars",0],["the tree and the slide",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagindir","caption":"Flagship lab: order ribbons, carry a string from a gap to a sofa, pull a string tight, compare two at a time, and pick a go-between that works.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_2XDGT5tei1":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The long hand goes once all the way round the clock. How much time has passed?","choices":["1 hour","1 minute","12 minutes"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many minutes does the long hand count in one turn?","explain":"One turn of the long hand is 60 minutes, which is 1 hour."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why does a day have 24 hours when the clock only shows 12?","choices":["The short hand goes round twice","The clock is broken","Some hours are skipped at night"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of morning and then afternoon and evening.","explain":"The short hand goes round once from midnight to midday and once more to midnight: 12 + 12 = 24 hours."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"How many hours are in {d} days?","answer":"24*d","hint":"Each day is 24 hours. Add 24 for every day.","explain":"{d} × 24 = {=24*d} hours.","params":{"d":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is longer?","choices":["70 minutes","1 hour","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change 1 hour into minutes first.","explain":"1 hour is 60 minutes, so 70 minutes is 10 minutes longer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Half an hour is how many minutes?","choices":["30","50","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Half of 60 minutes. Where is the long hand after half a turn?","explain":"An hour is 60 minutes, and half of 60 is 30. The long hand goes half way round, from 12 to 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You read for {m} minutes. How many more minutes until you have read for 1 hour?","answer":"60-m","hint":"1 hour is 60 minutes. How many more to get to 60?","explain":"60 − {m} = {=60-m}. Read for {=60-m} more minutes to make 1 hour.","params":{"m":[10,50]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Leo says there are 100 minutes in an hour. What is wrong?","choices":["An hour is 60 minutes","He should say 12 minutes","Nothing, he is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count round the clock in fives.","explain":"Time does not go in hundreds. Counting round the 12 numbers in fives reaches 60, so an hour is 60 minutes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"It is {h} o'clock in the morning. How many hours until midday, 12 o'clock?","answer":"12-h","hint":"Count on hour by hour from {h} to 12.","explain":"12 − {h} = {=12-h}. Midday is {=12-h} hours away.","params":{"h":[1,11]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which takes about 1 hour?","choices":["A swimming lesson","Clapping your hands once","A whole night's sleep"],"answer":0,"hint":"One clap takes a second, and a night's sleep takes many hours.","explain":"A swimming lesson is often about an hour. A clap takes a second, and sleep takes about 10 hours for a child."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many minutes are in 1 hour?","choices":["60","12","100"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the long hand going once round, 5 minutes for each of the 12 numbers.","explain":"The long hand goes once round in 1 hour. 12 numbers × 5 minutes = 60 minutes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"How many minutes are in {h} hours?","answer":"60*h","hint":"Each hour is 60 minutes. Add 60 for every hour.","explain":"{h} hours × 60 minutes = {=60*h} minutes.","params":{"h":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many hours are in 1 whole day and night?","choices":["24","12","60"],"answer":0,"hint":"The short hand goes round the clock twice in one day.","explain":"The short hand goes round twice: 12 hours and 12 more hours make 24 hours."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A film lasts 1 hour and {m} minutes. How many minutes is that?","answer":"60+m","hint":"Change the hour into 60 minutes, then add the extra minutes.","explain":"1 hour is 60 minutes. 60 + {m} = {=60+m} minutes.","params":{"m":[5,50]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"The long hand points at {n}. How many minutes past the hour is it?","answer":"5*n","hint":"Count in fives from 12: 1 is 5, 2 is 10, 3 is 15.","explain":"Each number is 5 minutes, so {n} × 5 = {=5*n} minutes past.","params":{"n":[1,11]}}],"explain":{"kid":["The long hand on a clock counts minutes. When it goes all the way round once, 60 minutes have passed. That is 1 hour.","There are only 12 numbers on the clock. Each number is 5 minutes for the long hand. Count in fives: 5, 10, 15 … all the way to 60.","The short hand counts hours. It goes round once from night to midday. Then it goes round again to night. Twice round is 24 hours. That is 1 whole day.","Now you can work things out. 2 hours is 60 and 60 more. That makes 120 minutes. Half an hour is 30 minutes."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. This is the Year 2 statutory aim in the National Curriculum in England: know the number of minutes in an hour and the number of hours in a day. It sits alongside telling the time to five minutes, which is US Common Core 2.MD.7. Key words: minute, hour, day, minute hand, hour hand, half an hour, o'clock, midday, midnight.","Two facts carry the topic: 1 hour = 60 minutes and 1 day = 24 hours. Children meet them through the clock face. The minute hand makes one turn per hour, and the hour hand makes two turns per day, which is why a 12-hour face still shows a 24-hour day.","Common mistakes: saying an hour has 100 minutes, as if time were metric; saying an hour has 12 minutes because there are 12 numbers; saying a day has 12 hours because the clock shows 12; and reading the long hand on 3 as 3 minutes rather than 15. Watch also for adding 2 hours as 62 minutes.","In the lab your child winds the long hand once round while 60 beads fill. They count round the 12 numbers in fives, move the short hand through 24 hours of day and night, put 1-hour bars on a minute line and sort six times as less than, exactly or more than 1 hour."]},"familyTip":"Set a kitchen timer for 60 minutes when your child starts a film or a game, and look at the clock together: where is the long hand now, and where will it be when the timer rings? At bedtime, count back on your fingers how many hours ago they woke up.","keyIdea":"There are 60 minutes in 1 hour: the long hand goes once round the clock. There are 24 hours in 1 day: the short hand goes round twice, once for the 12 hours from midnight to midday and once more for the 12 hours back to midnight. Each number on the clock stands for 5 minutes on the long hand, so counting in fives round all 12 numbers makes 60. Use the facts to work out times: 2 hours is 60 + 60 = 120 minutes, and 90 minutes is more than an hour.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Would you time it in minutes or in hours? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Minutes","Hours"],"items":[["Brushing your teeth",0],["A night's sleep",1],["Putting on your shoes",0],["A school day",1],["Eating a snack",0],["A long car trip",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghr60","caption":"Flagship lab: wind the long hand round as 60 beads fill, count in fives, run a 24-hour day, lay hour bars on a minute line and sort times.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_AQo4u7O4sM":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"What is {r} × ({a} + {b})?","answer":"r*(a+b)","hint":"Multiply {r} by each number in the brackets, then add.","explain":"{r} × {a} = {=r*a} and {r} × {b} = {=r*b}, so the total is {=r*(a+b)}.","params":{"r":[2,7],"a":[4,8],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"{r} × {a} + {r} × {b} = {r} × what?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Both parts are {r} tall. Put the two widths together.","explain":"The widths {a} and {b} join to make {=a+b}, so it is {r} × {=a+b}.","params":{"r":[3,6],"a":[2,7],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia works out 7 × 12 as 7 × 10 + 2 = 72. What went wrong?","choices":["She did not multiply the 2 by 7","She should add 7 + 12","Nothing, 72 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Draw it: the small part is 7 tall and 2 wide.","explain":"The small part is 7 × 2 = 14, not 2. So 7 × 12 = 70 + 14 = 84."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which sum gives the area of a 6 by 15 rectangle?","choices":["6 × 10 + 6 × 5","6 × 10 × 6 × 5","6 + 10 + 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cut 15 into 10 and 5. The two parts sit side by side, so add them.","explain":"6 × 10 = 60 and 6 × 5 = 30. 60 + 30 = 90, which is 6 × 15."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A garden is {r} m wide. It has a {a} m long lawn and a {b} m long patio side by side. What is the total area in square metres?","answer":"r*(a+b)","hint":"Find the lawn area and the patio area. Then add.","explain":"Lawn {r} × {a} = {=r*a}, patio {r} × {b} = {=r*b}. Total {=r*(a+b)} square metres.","params":{"r":[3,5],"a":[6,9],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A 5 by 8 rectangle is cut into two parts. One part is 5 by 3. What is the other part?","choices":["5 by 5","3 by 8","5 by 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"The cut runs top to bottom, so the height stays 5. What is left of 8?","explain":"8 − 3 = 5, so the other part is 5 by 5. 15 + 25 = 40 = 5 × 8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is easiest for working out 8 × 14 in your head?","choices":["8 × 10 + 8 × 4","8 × 7 + 8 × 7","8 × 13 + 8 × 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"All three are correct. Which has a part that is times 10?","explain":"8 × 10 = 80 is quick, and 8 × 4 = 32. So 8 × 14 = 112. The other cuts work too, but are harder."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"{r} × 2{b} = {r} × 20 + {r} × {b}. What is the answer?","answer":"r*(20+b)","hint":"Work out {r} × 20 first, then {r} × {b}, then add.","explain":"{r} × 20 = {=20*r} and {r} × {b} = {=r*b}. Total {=r*(20+b)}.","params":{"r":[2,5],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Tom says a 4 by 7 rectangle has area 11. What did he do?","choices":["He added the sides instead of multiplying","He multiplied correctly","He counted the perimeter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 4 + 7 or 4 × 7 the number of squares?","explain":"4 + 7 = 11 adds the sides. There are 4 rows of 7 squares, so the area is 4 × 7 = 28."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A rectangle is {r} squares tall and {a} + {b} squares long. What is its area in square units?","answer":"r*(a+b)","hint":"Find {r} × {a} and {r} × {b}, then add the two parts.","explain":"{r} × {a} = {=r*a} and {r} × {b} = {=r*b}. {=r*a} + {=r*b} = {=r*(a+b)} square units.","params":{"r":[2,6],"a":[3,7],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is the same as 5 × (8 + 2)?","choices":["5 × 8 + 5 × 2","5 × 8 + 2","5 + 8 + 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both parts of the rectangle are 5 tall. Multiply each part by 5.","explain":"5 × (8 + 2) = 5 × 8 + 5 × 2 = 40 + 10 = 50, which is 5 × 10."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Use 10 and {b}: what is {r} × 1{b}?","answer":"r*(10+b)","hint":"Cut 1{b} into 10 and {b}. Work out {r} × 10 and {r} × {b}.","explain":"{r} × 10 = {=10*r} and {r} × {b} = {=r*b}. {=10*r} + {=r*b} = {=r*(10+b)}.","params":{"r":[3,8],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A 4 by 9 rectangle is cut into a 4 by 5 part and a 4 by 4 part. What is the total area?","choices":["20 + 16 = 36","20 + 4 = 24","Less than 36, because of the cut"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the area of each part. A cut does not lose any squares.","explain":"4 × 5 = 20 and 4 × 4 = 16, so 20 + 16 = 36. That is 4 × 9."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which area model shows 3 × (6 + 4)?","choices":["3 tall, cut into 6 and 4","6 tall, cut into 3 and 4","3 tall and 6 long, no cut"],"answer":0,"hint":"The number outside the brackets is the side both parts share.","explain":"Both parts are 3 tall. The widths are 6 and 4, so the whole is 3 by 10."}],"explain":{"kid":["Area is how many squares cover a shape. A rectangle 3 squares tall and 6 long has 3 rows of 6. That is 3 × 6 = 18 squares.","Now cut it with one straight line from top to bottom. One part is 3 by 4. The other part is 3 by 2. That gives 12 squares and 6 squares. 12 + 6 = 18. No square went away.","So 3 × 6 is the same as 3 × 4 + 3 × 2. We write it as 3 × (4 + 2) = 3 × 4 + 3 × 2. The 3 is the height. Both parts have it, so both parts get times 3.","This helps with big facts. For 6 × 13, cut 13 into 10 and 3. Then 6 × 10 = 60 and 6 × 3 = 18. Add them: 60 + 18 = 78. Do not forget to times the small part too."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. This is US Common Core 3.MD.C.7c, use tiling to show that the area of a rectangle with whole-number sides a and b + c is the sum of a × b and a × c, and 3.OA.B.5, apply the distributive property. It supports partitioning in Year 3 and Year 4 multiplication in the National Curriculum in England. Key words: area, square unit, tiling, area model, partition, distributive property, brackets.","The area model is the bridge from counting tiles to written multiplication. A rectangle split into two parts shows why each part must be multiplied by the shared side. Later the same picture explains the grid method, long multiplication and expanding brackets such as 3(x + 2) = 3x + 6.","Common mistakes: multiplying only the first part, so 6 × 13 becomes 6 × 10 + 3 = 63; adding the sides instead of multiplying them, so a 3 by 6 rectangle seems to be 9; thinking a cut loses some area; multiplying the two parts together instead of adding them; and using a number inside the brackets as the shared side.","In the lab your child lays columns of tiles over a 3 by 6 rectangle, then drags a cut line and sees 3 × 4 + 3 × 2 stay at 18. They try three cuts, split 6 × 13 at ten to get 60 + 18, see why 6 × 10 + 3 leaves out 15 tiles, and build area models for three bracket equations."]},"familyTip":"Use a bar of chocolate, a tray of ice cubes or squared paper. Snap or draw a line across it and ask: how many squares in each part, and in all? Then try a shopping sum like 4 packs of 12: 4 × 10 is 40, 4 × 2 is 8, so 48.","keyIdea":"Cut a rectangle into two parts and no tiles are lost. A rectangle 3 tall and 4 + 2 long has area 3 × 6 = 18, and its parts have 3 × 4 = 12 and 3 × 2 = 6, so 3 × (4 + 2) = 3 × 4 + 3 × 2. This is the distributive property. It turns a hard times fact into two easy ones: 6 × 13 = 6 × 10 + 6 × 3 = 60 + 18 = 78. Both parts share the same height, so the number outside the brackets multiplies each part.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each sum give the same area as 4 × 13? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Same as 4 × 13","Not the same"],"items":[["4 × 10 + 4 × 3",0],["4 × 10 + 3",1],["4 × 8 + 4 × 5",0],["4 + 13",1],["40 + 12",0],["4 × 10 × 4 × 3",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdistrib","caption":"Flagship lab: tile a 3 by 6 rectangle, cut it without losing a tile, try every cut, split 6 × 13 at ten, and build area models for bracket equations.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_mayItsxMUu":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where should one end of the object go on the ruler?","choices":["At 0","At 1","At the end of the ruler"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at the very first mark, before 1.","explain":"Line one end up with 0. Then the number at the other end is the length."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A twig ends close to 9, just before it. To the nearest cm it is…","choices":["9 cm","8 cm","10 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which mark is closer to the end of the twig?","explain":"The end is nearly at 9, so to the nearest whole centimetre it is 9 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"On a line plot, what does each X stand for?","choices":["One object that was measured","One centimetre","The longest object"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the Xs to count the objects.","explain":"Each X is one object, placed above its length."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A ribbon starts at {s} cm and ends at {e} cm on a ruler. How long is it in cm?","answer":"e-s","hint":"Take the start mark away from the end mark to find the gap between them.","explain":"The length is the gap from start to end: {e} − {s} = {=e-s} cm.","params":{"s":[2,5],"e":[10,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"No leaf is 10 cm long. Should 10 still be on the line plot's scale?","choices":["Yes, every whole number gets a place","No, leave it out","Only if you have room"],"answer":0,"hint":"A line plot is a number line with equal steps.","explain":"Keep every whole number, equally spaced, even ones with no Xs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The tallest stack of Xs is above 7. What does that tell you?","choices":["7 cm is the most common length","The longest leaf is 7 cm","7 leaves were measured"],"answer":0,"hint":"A tall stack means many objects had that length.","explain":"The tallest stack is the length that came up most often: 7 cm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A line plot has 1 X above 5, {a} Xs above 6 and 3 Xs above 7. How many objects were measured?","answer":"1+a+3","hint":"Each X is one object, so add up all the Xs on the plot.","explain":"1 + {a} + 3 = {=a+4} objects.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam's line plot goes 4, 5, 6, 8, 9. What is wrong?","choices":["7 is missing, so the steps are not equal","Nothing is wrong","It should start at 9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count up in ones. Is any number skipped?","explain":"The scale skips 7. A line plot needs every whole number in equal steps."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which X on a line plot shows the shortest leaf?","choices":["The one furthest to the left","The one at the top of the tallest stack","The one furthest to the right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Numbers on the line get bigger as you go right.","explain":"Smaller lengths are on the left, so the X furthest left is the shortest leaf."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A crayon goes from 0 to 8 on a ruler. How long is it?","choices":["8 cm","9 cm","0 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"When one end is at 0, the number at the other end is the length.","explain":"It starts at 0 and ends at 8, so it is 8 cm long."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A pencil starts at the {a} cm mark and ends at the {b} cm mark. How long is it in cm?","answer":"b-a","hint":"The length is the gap between the two marks: take the start away from the end.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a}, so the pencil is {=b-a} cm long.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[9,14]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A shell ends just past 5, not near 6. To the nearest cm it is…","choices":["5 cm","6 cm","4 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which whole mark is the end closer to?","explain":"The end is much closer to the 5 mark than the 6 mark, so it is about 5 cm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A line plot has {x} Xs above 7 cm and 2 Xs above 8 cm. How many objects are 7 cm or 8 cm long?","answer":"x+2","hint":"Each X is one object. Add the Xs in both stacks.","explain":"{x} + 2 = {=x+2} objects.","params":{"x":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Three leaves are each 6 cm long. How should the line plot show them?","choices":["3 Xs stacked above 6","1 X above 6","1 X above 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each leaf gets its own X.","explain":"Every leaf gets one X, so three 6 cm leaves make a stack of 3 Xs above 6."}],"explain":{"kid":["To measure how long something is, put one end at 0 on the ruler. Then look at the other end. The number there is the length. If you start at 2 instead, the end number is too big. A leaf from 2 to 9 is only 9 − 2 = 7 cm long.","Ends do not always land on a mark. Then you round to the nearest whole centimetre. Look at which mark the end is closer to. A leaf that ends at 6 and a little bit is about 6 cm. A leaf that ends nearly at 7 is about 7 cm.","A line plot shows lots of measurements at once. Draw a number line with every whole centimetre, in equal steps. Put one X above the length of each leaf. Two leaves that are both 6 cm get two Xs, one on top of the other, above 6.","Now you can read the plot. The tallest stack shows the most common length. The X furthest to the right is the longest leaf, and the one furthest to the left is the shortest. Keep every number on the line, even if it has no Xs."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. This matches Common Core grade 2, 2.MD.9: generate measurement data by measuring lengths of several objects to the nearest whole unit, and show the measurements on a line plot whose horizontal scale is marked in whole-number units. In England it fits Year 2 measurement (choose and use units, measure length to the nearest cm) and statistics. Key words: length, centimetre, ruler, zero, nearest whole unit, data, line plot, scale, X mark, most common, longest, shortest.","A line plot (also called a dot plot) is a number line with a mark for each data value. In grade 2 the scale goes up in whole units, and each object gets exactly one X. Rounding to the nearest centimetre means choosing the nearer whole mark. In the lab, the rounded leaves are 4.8, 6.3 and 7.6 cm, so none sits exactly on a half.","Common mistakes: starting at 1 or at the end of the ruler and reading the end number as the length; always rounding up; drawing one X for a length instead of one per object; reading the numbers on the scale as counts; leaving out numbers with no Xs, so the steps are uneven; and mixing up the most common length with the longest.","In the lab your child slides a leaf from 2 to 0 and sees that 2 to 9 is 7 cm. They round three leaves, stack ten lengths as Xs, and read the plot to find the most common, longest and shortest lengths. Then they count the leaves longer than 7 cm and fix a scale that skipped 7."]},"familyTip":"Collect about ten leaves, pencils or toy cars. Measure each one to the nearest centimetre and write the numbers down. Draw a number line with every whole centimetre from the shortest to the longest, then put one X above each length. Ask: which length came up most? How many are longer than 7 cm?","keyIdea":"To measure a length, line up one end of the object with 0 on the ruler, then read the mark at the other end. If the end falls between two marks, choose the whole centimetre it is closest to. To show many measurements at once, draw a line plot: a number line marked in equal steps of whole centimetres, with one X above the length of each object. Xs for the same length stack up.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each measurement done right? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Done right","A slip"],"items":[["Start the ruler at 0",0],["Start the ruler at 1",1],["6.8 cm rounds to 7 cm",0],["6.2 cm rounds to 7 cm",1],["Leave out numbers with no Xs",1],["One X for each object",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglplot","caption":"Flagship lab: slide a leaf to 0, round three leaves to the nearest cm, plot ten lengths as Xs, read the plot and fix a broken scale.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt__N55B7u7HD":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many seconds make 1 minute?","choices":["60","100","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the 10-second scoops that fill a 1-minute timer.","explain":"6 scoops of 10 seconds fill the timer, so 60 seconds make 1 minute, not 100."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A 1-minute timer has {=10*n} seconds of sand in it so far. How many more seconds of sand will fill it?","answer":"60-10*n","hint":"A full minute is 60 seconds. Count on in tens from {=10*n} to 60.","explain":"{=10*n} seconds and {=60-10*n} more seconds make 60 seconds, which is 1 minute.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which unit fits eating a snack?","choices":["Minutes","Seconds","Hours"],"answer":0,"hint":"A snack is longer than a blink but much shorter than a night of sleep.","explain":"Eating a snack takes a few minutes: longer than seconds, shorter than hours."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"When should you stop the stopwatch?","choices":["When the job ends","When it shows 10","Before the job ends"],"answer":0,"hint":"You want the stopwatch to show how long the whole job took.","explain":"Stop it when the job ends. Then the number shows how long the job took."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Sam turns a 1-minute sand timer {n} times while he reads. Then he turns it 1 more time. How many minutes did he read?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Each turn is 1 minute. Count all the turns, then add the last one.","explain":"{n} turns and 1 more is {=n+1} turns, so Sam read for {=n+1} minutes.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does a stopwatch measure?","choices":["How long something takes","What day it is","How heavy something is"],"answer":0,"hint":"You press Start when a job begins and Stop when it ends.","explain":"A stopwatch measures how long something takes, by counting the seconds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Tom says, 'My swim took 3 o'clock.' What is wrong?","choices":["O'clock tells when, not how long","Swims always take hours","Nothing is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 3 o'clock a length of time, or a time on the clock?","explain":"3 o'clock is when something happens. To say how long, Tom needs seconds, minutes or hours."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is longer, 1 minute or 1 second?","choices":["1 minute","1 second","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many seconds fit in 1 minute?","explain":"1 minute is longer. It is 60 seconds, so 60 seconds fit inside it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which would you time in hours?","choices":["A long car trip","A sneeze","Putting on a sock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hours are for long things. Which one lasts a long time?","explain":"A long car trip lasts hours. A sneeze takes a second and a sock takes seconds."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which unit would you use to time a blink?","choices":["Seconds","Minutes","Hours"],"answer":0,"hint":"A blink is over very fast. Which unit is the shortest?","explain":"A blink takes about a second, so we time it in seconds, the shortest unit."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A 1-minute sand timer runs out {n} times while you tidy your room. How many minutes did you tidy?","answer":"n","hint":"Each time the sand runs out, 1 minute has gone by. Count the turns.","explain":"{n} turns of a 1-minute timer is {n} minutes of tidying.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Each scoop of sand is 10 seconds. You put {n} scoops in the timer. How many seconds is that?","answer":"10*n","hint":"Count in tens, one ten for each scoop: 10, 20, 30 …","explain":"{n} scoops of 10 seconds is {=10*n} seconds.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A stopwatch shows 7 when the race ends. How long did the race take?","choices":["7 seconds","7 o'clock","7 hours"],"answer":0,"hint":"A stopwatch counts seconds. Is o'clock a time of day or a length of time?","explain":"The stopwatch counted 7 seconds, so the race took 7 seconds. O'clock is a time of day."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which of these takes the longest time?","choices":["A school day","Brushing your teeth","One clap"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one lasts hours? Which lasts minutes? Which lasts a second?","explain":"A school day lasts hours, brushing teeth takes minutes and a clap takes about a second."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some things take a short time and some take a long time. We use units to say how long. A second is very short. A blink takes about a second. A minute is longer. Brushing your teeth takes about 2 minutes. An hour is longer still. A night of sleep takes many hours.","60 seconds make 1 minute. You can count to 60 in tens: 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60. That is 6 tens. It is not 100. A sand timer that lasts 1 minute holds 60 seconds of sand.","A stopwatch counts the seconds. Press Start when the job starts. Press Stop when the job ends. Then read the number. If it says 8, the job took 8 seconds.","When you write it down, say how long it took and add the unit: 8 seconds, 2 minutes, 3 hours. Do not write 2 o'clock. O'clock tells you when something happens, not how long it takes."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 6. This matches the National Curriculum in England for Year 1 Measurement: measure and begin to record time (hours, minutes, seconds), and compare, describe and solve practical problems for time. It links to CCSS K.MD.A.1 and K.MD.A.2 (describe and compare measurable attributes) and leads into Year 2 work on minutes in an hour. Key words: time, duration, second, minute, hour, stopwatch, sand timer, longer, shorter, how long.","The aim at this stage is a feel for the size of each unit and the habit of recording a duration with a number and a unit. Children should know that 1 minute is 60 seconds, but they are not yet expected to convert between units or read a clock to the minute. Sand timers are a good first tool because the whole minute is visible, and a stopwatch shows seconds counting up in real time.","Common mistakes: thinking a minute is 100 seconds, because they count in hundreds elsewhere; choosing a unit that is far too short or too long, such as 'brushing teeth takes 2 hours'; stopping a stopwatch early or late, so the reading is not how long the job took; and mixing up a duration with a time of day, as in 'my swim took 3 o'clock'. The lab, practice and challenge each target these slips.","In the lab your child sorts six everyday jobs into seconds, minutes and hours, fills a 1-minute sand timer with scoops of 10 seconds until it is full at 60, times a frog's hops with a stopwatch and must press Stop as it lands, picks the right words to record three timings, and turns a 1-minute sand timer twice to brush for 2 minutes. The sand in the last step is sped up and says so."]},"familyTip":"Time some everyday jobs together with a phone stopwatch or a kitchen timer: putting on shoes, tidying the toys, a bath. Before you start, guess: seconds or minutes? Then say the result out loud, such as 'That took about 3 minutes'.","keyIdea":"We measure how long something takes in seconds, minutes or hours. Seconds are short, like a blink. Minutes are longer, like brushing your teeth. Hours are longest, like a night of sleep. 60 seconds make 1 minute. A stopwatch counts the seconds while something happens, and a sand timer shows when a set time is up. When we write it down, we say how long it took, such as 'about 2 minutes', not a time of day like '2 o'clock'.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Seconds, minutes or hours? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["seconds","minutes","hours"],"items":[["A sneeze",0],["Jumping once",0],["Eating lunch",1],["Washing your hands well",0],["Brushing your teeth",1],["A night of sleep",2],["A long car trip",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsecs","caption":"Flagship lab: sort jobs by unit, fill a 1-minute sand timer 10 seconds at a time, time a frog with a stopwatch, write times down, brush for 2 minutes.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
